WEBVTT

1
00:00:24,916 --> 00:00:26,416
Come on, guys. Hurry up.

2
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,041
The audition is in three hours.
Why did we have to get up at 6:00?

3
00:00:30,666 --> 00:00:33,958
-Do you think Take That slept until noon?
-I'm so tired.

4
00:00:34,041 --> 00:00:36,541
'Cause someone watched
the Grammys yesterday.

5
00:00:36,625 --> 00:00:38,291
-Yes.
-It was a group decision.

6
00:00:38,375 --> 00:00:39,625
-Yeah, yeah.
-Exactly!

7
00:00:39,708 --> 00:00:42,250
-Actually, where's Oli?
-Good question.

8
00:00:43,291 --> 00:00:44,916
-There he is.
-Morning.

9
00:00:45,666 --> 00:00:49,583
-Sorry, there was a long lineup.
-You were flirting with that girl again.

10
00:00:49,666 --> 00:00:52,958
-I was not. She was flirting with me.
-Yeah, sure.

11
00:00:53,041 --> 00:00:55,500
Oh, man, no pink sprinkles.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,166
-They're Peter's favorite.
-Sorry, they were sold out.

13
00:01:00,125 --> 00:01:02,166
Hopefully, he's in a good mood.

14
00:01:05,916 --> 00:01:08,041
Sorry, Peter. No pink sprinkles today.

15
00:01:11,375 --> 00:01:12,291
Thank you.

16
00:01:13,416 --> 00:01:15,125
-Come on, guys.
-Yes, all right.

17
00:01:49,375 --> 00:01:51,875
Let's stop whining
and pull ourselves together.

18
00:01:51,958 --> 00:01:54,666
Let's really crank up the bass
at rehearsal.

19
00:01:55,166 --> 00:01:57,333
That's the right attitude.

20
00:02:00,291 --> 00:02:03,916
-Sleep is overrated.
-You're such a weirdo.

21
00:02:12,375 --> 00:02:15,000
<i>All of this time</i>
<i>Make it all make sense</i>

22
00:02:15,083 --> 00:02:18,916
<i>I'm going round and round</i>
<i>in circles again</i>

23
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,666
<i>It's a cataclysmic ride into the unknown</i>

24
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:26,291
<i>Impossible to synchronize</i>
<i>But let's go</i>

25
00:02:26,375 --> 00:02:29,250
<i>All of these lights can we recompare</i>

26
00:02:29,333 --> 00:02:32,708
<i>I'm going higher and higher</i>
<i>From Earth into space</i>

27
00:02:32,791 --> 00:02:36,375
<i>It's an existential search</i>
<i>In how it hurts</i>

28
00:02:37,125 --> 00:02:38,333
Stop, no, no.

29
00:02:38,916 --> 00:02:39,750
Guys!

30
00:02:40,625 --> 00:02:43,708
-What's wrong?
-We can't perform the song like this.

31
00:02:43,791 --> 00:02:45,041
What do you mean?

32
00:02:45,125 --> 00:02:47,708
Something's missing.
The song lacks magic. It's got nothing.

33
00:02:47,791 --> 00:02:50,000
-Not again, bro.
-Ben, the showcase is coming up.

34
00:02:50,083 --> 00:02:53,083
-We need to make some progress.
-Guys, these lyrics. Seriously.

35
00:02:53,166 --> 00:02:54,583
We keep starting over.

36
00:02:54,666 --> 00:02:56,875
-Bro, the lyrics are great.
-No, Matteo!

37
00:02:57,958 --> 00:02:59,625
Just give me a few minutes, okay?

38
00:02:59,708 --> 00:03:01,375
Unbelievable.

39
00:03:01,458 --> 00:03:04,291
-Let's just take a break.
-Any moment now…

40
00:03:05,416 --> 00:03:09,208
If we start all over again now
and do everything from scratch…

41
00:03:09,291 --> 00:03:12,166
All the lyrics, the choreo, the chords.

42
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:14,875
How many times has it been this month?

43
00:03:14,958 --> 00:03:18,250
We can't keep scrapping the song
and starting from scratch.

44
00:03:18,333 --> 00:03:22,000
He's writing in his notebook again.
Nothing's gonna come of it.

45
00:03:22,083 --> 00:03:23,875
-He's changing everything.
-I don't get it.

46
00:03:23,958 --> 00:03:25,333
-Hi there.
-Hi.

47
00:03:29,333 --> 00:03:32,458
-It's getting serious now, isn't it?
-Yes. Totally.

48
00:03:33,333 --> 00:03:37,666
If you mess up now, then you don't stand
a chance later in front of the pros.

49
00:03:38,583 --> 00:03:39,416
Yes.

50
00:03:39,916 --> 00:03:41,625
Hi, Sky. What's up?

51
00:03:41,958 --> 00:03:42,791
Hi.

52
00:03:43,291 --> 00:03:44,208
Hi, Sky.

53
00:03:44,291 --> 00:03:46,250
-Your bag, Ben.
-How's the song coming along?

54
00:03:46,333 --> 00:03:48,666
-I'm a little nervous.
-How are things, Sky?

55
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:50,125
All good, and you?

56
00:03:50,625 --> 00:03:53,625
-Ready to wow them all?
-We'll see.

57
00:03:53,708 --> 00:03:54,833
100%.

58
00:03:54,916 --> 00:03:57,000
How did Matteo describe your voice again?

59
00:03:57,083 --> 00:03:59,541
Like if Beyoncé and DaBaby
had a love child.

60
00:03:59,625 --> 00:04:01,291
Debbie Harry, not DaBaby.

61
00:04:01,375 --> 00:04:02,541
Oh, God.

62
00:04:03,583 --> 00:04:04,541
Seriously.

63
00:04:08,958 --> 00:04:12,375
-I'll go run through the lyrics again.
-Totally.

64
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,708
-You're gonna do great.
-Good luck.

65
00:04:14,791 --> 00:04:15,875
Later, guys.

66
00:04:19,958 --> 00:04:21,541
-Good morning.
-Are you stupid?

67
00:04:21,625 --> 00:04:22,458
Morning.

68
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:26,083
-Good morning, everyone.
-Morning.

69
00:04:26,166 --> 00:04:30,208
The showcase is in a few days, so now's
your chance to show what you've got.

70
00:04:30,291 --> 00:04:33,375
Remember, several music professionals
will be there.

71
00:04:33,458 --> 00:04:36,750
-Wait, Ms. Robinson is in <i>Synk</i>?
-She has more Grammys than Taylor Swift.

72
00:04:36,833 --> 00:04:38,000
They're not playing around.

73
00:04:38,083 --> 00:04:41,250
Yeah, bro.
Working with her would be so wild.

74
00:04:41,333 --> 00:04:44,791
{\an8}They want to see artists
who are ready to give it their all.

75
00:04:44,875 --> 00:04:47,000
Once you're on stage,
there are no more excuses.

76
00:04:47,083 --> 00:04:49,041
-Okay?
-Okay.

77
00:04:51,166 --> 00:04:55,791
<i>I can see you in the corner of the room</i>

78
00:04:57,708 --> 00:05:03,083
<i>When you're near</i>
<i>My heart beats out of tune</i>

79
00:05:03,166 --> 00:05:06,583
<i>Oh, I wish you'd notice</i>

80
00:05:06,666 --> 00:05:09,625
<i>The way I light up, too</i>

81
00:05:13,166 --> 00:05:16,916
<i>Somewhere around</i>

82
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:20,375
<i>A world of endless grace</i>

83
00:05:21,166 --> 00:05:23,291
<i>I'm more than just a face</i>

84
00:05:24,541 --> 00:05:26,916
<i>Just another face </i>

85
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:43,666
Skyler.

86
00:05:44,416 --> 00:05:45,416
Skyler, wait.

87
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:47,916
-Just forget it.
-Please wait.

88
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:52,208
I'm sorry.

89
00:05:53,083 --> 00:05:57,416
I had to work on the lyrics,
and the bridge isn't quite right yet.

90
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:00,333
-The boys don't understand that I…
-Ben.

91
00:06:01,166 --> 00:06:02,416
It's okay.

92
00:06:03,083 --> 00:06:06,875
It's just, your world, this perfectionism…

93
00:06:06,958 --> 00:06:08,125
I'm done.

94
00:06:09,041 --> 00:06:10,166
That's it for us.

95
00:06:10,666 --> 00:06:13,541
I hope that you will feel
something again one day.

96
00:06:13,625 --> 00:06:14,458
But…

97
00:06:18,250 --> 00:06:19,666
Come on, we're up.

98
00:06:21,166 --> 00:06:22,000
Ben!

99
00:06:22,541 --> 00:06:23,458
Come on.

100
00:06:31,666 --> 00:06:35,291
Okay. Our song is unfortunately
not quite finished yet.

101
00:06:35,375 --> 00:06:38,166
That's right,
but it will be ready for the showcase.

102
00:06:38,250 --> 00:06:39,500
Are you okay?

103
00:06:40,666 --> 00:06:43,375
We are 5G,
and this is "Gotta Make It Work."

104
00:06:48,041 --> 00:06:51,083
<i>I hope that you will feel</i>
<i>something again one day.</i>

105
00:06:56,833 --> 00:06:58,125
Hey, what's going on?

106
00:06:58,208 --> 00:07:00,500
-Ben.
-Where are you going?

107
00:07:07,833 --> 00:07:08,666
Ben?

108
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:12,250
Come on, dude.
Let us at least talk to you.

109
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:18,333
Just chill out.
You just had a little blackout.

110
00:07:18,416 --> 00:07:20,750
-It happens.
-You don't get it.

111
00:07:22,250 --> 00:07:25,750
-That's your problem exactly. You're…
-Just go away!

112
00:07:27,625 --> 00:07:29,916
That's it, guys. I'm out.

113
00:07:47,916 --> 00:07:49,416
Come on, guys. Leave him be.

114
00:08:16,583 --> 00:08:17,500
This sucks.

115
00:08:20,291 --> 00:08:21,666
Come on, guys.

116
00:08:21,750 --> 00:08:24,166
-It is still not working.
-Damn it.

117
00:08:24,250 --> 00:08:27,375
-How will we do this without him?
-We have to be honest with ourselves.

118
00:08:27,458 --> 00:08:29,166
It doesn't work without him. Fact.

119
00:08:29,250 --> 00:08:32,750
We can't just throw it all away now.
Then this will all have been for nothing.

120
00:08:32,833 --> 00:08:35,125
-Let's try again.
-You, I…

121
00:08:35,958 --> 00:08:37,916
-I need a break first.
-Me, too.

122
00:08:40,416 --> 00:08:41,875
Well, this sucks.

123
00:08:49,583 --> 00:08:51,958
A follower just sent me
a link to a TikTok page.

124
00:08:52,041 --> 00:08:54,208
With these really cool
vintage '90s B-sides.

125
00:08:54,291 --> 00:08:57,750
-Really? Your solution is old B-sides?
-Listen to it. It's awesome.

126
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,458
<i>I still hear you</i>

127
00:09:03,958 --> 00:09:07,916
<i>In the midnight air</i>

128
00:09:09,083 --> 00:09:12,750
<i>Your voice is the wind</i>
<i>It's the sound</i>

129
00:09:12,833 --> 00:09:15,000
<i>Of a whispered prayer</i>

130
00:09:20,333 --> 00:09:23,416
Honestly, it's way cooler
than anything we're doing right now.

131
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:26,250
-How do you make something like that?
-I wish I knew.

132
00:09:27,583 --> 00:09:28,750
Hey, losers!

133
00:09:32,916 --> 00:09:34,291
What do you want, Alisha?

134
00:09:35,083 --> 00:09:36,833
Aren't you called 5G?

135
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:40,875
-So why are there only 4Gs?
-Stop staring!

136
00:09:40,958 --> 00:09:43,750
-Shut up, Oli.
-You're nothing without Ben.

137
00:09:48,583 --> 00:09:50,208
Well, guys, she's not wrong.

138
00:09:52,083 --> 00:09:53,291
Chris.

139
00:09:54,333 --> 00:09:55,458
What's going on here?

140
00:09:57,875 --> 00:10:02,458
<i>The big Heartville University showcase</i>
<i>is scheduled for tonight.</i>

141
00:10:02,541 --> 00:10:06,625
<i>Who will blow the jury away?</i>
<i>Who will go down in music history?</i>

142
00:10:06,708 --> 00:10:09,916
<i>But this date also brings up</i>
<i>some bad memories for Heartville.</i>

143
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,000
<i>Thirty years ago, on June 6, 1995,</i>
<i>at exactly 6:00 p.m.,</i>

144
00:10:14,083 --> 00:10:18,375
<i>-the elevator cable snapped…</i>
<i>-Today is the showcase. We need you.</i>

145
00:10:18,458 --> 00:10:21,500
<i>-Please call.</i>
<i>-…and Daniel Weber died.</i>

146
00:10:21,583 --> 00:10:24,458
<i>Out of respect, the elevator</i>
<i>was not used for decades,</i>

147
00:10:24,541 --> 00:10:29,041
<i>but was recently put back into service.</i>
<i>A simple brass plaque commemorates this…</i>

148
00:10:29,125 --> 00:10:31,833
Hopefully, his dad will be done
lecturing him soon. We gotta go.

149
00:10:31,916 --> 00:10:32,791
Yeah, man.

150
00:10:32,875 --> 00:10:35,208
You said that already. One could argue…

151
00:10:35,291 --> 00:10:36,875
-Bye, Dad.
-Bye, darling.

152
00:10:37,958 --> 00:10:39,958
Bye. Ciao, Matteo.

153
00:10:40,041 --> 00:10:42,791
-Bye… Bye.
-…but it keeps happening.

154
00:10:42,875 --> 00:10:44,375
"Ciao, Matteo"?

155
00:10:44,958 --> 00:10:47,041
What did I say about rehearsing here?

156
00:10:48,458 --> 00:10:51,666
Yes. I'm sorry. It won't happen again.

157
00:10:52,583 --> 00:10:53,500
Never mind.

158
00:10:54,791 --> 00:10:57,750
You won't have time for that anyway.
Do you know why?

159
00:11:03,541 --> 00:11:06,500
You've been accepted at Heartville.
To study medicine.

160
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:08,083
Are you happy?

161
00:11:08,916 --> 00:11:11,541
Yes, but the year isn't over yet.

162
00:11:14,833 --> 00:11:15,666
Chris.

163
00:11:16,166 --> 00:11:20,083
I know how much music
and all of this means to you, but…

164
00:11:22,208 --> 00:11:23,625
What if it doesn't work out?

165
00:11:25,291 --> 00:11:26,541
This is a safe prospect.

166
00:11:27,041 --> 00:11:30,875
I don't want you to throw this away
and regret it later. Do you understand?

167
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:36,250
Then I just have to make sure
that it does go somewhere.

168
00:11:37,041 --> 00:11:38,125
You're…

169
00:11:39,666 --> 00:11:42,208
When I was young, I never had
garbage like that in my head.

170
00:11:42,291 --> 00:11:44,833
Please, don't start that again.
I have to go.

171
00:11:44,916 --> 00:11:47,625
Your interview with Dr. Mehackerberg
is this afternoon.

172
00:11:47,708 --> 00:11:48,875
Yes.

173
00:11:48,958 --> 00:11:50,625
-Be on time.
-Yes.

174
00:11:50,708 --> 00:11:51,750
-What's up?
-Nothing.

175
00:11:51,833 --> 00:11:53,583
He's just lecturing me again.

176
00:11:53,666 --> 00:11:56,916
We've invited some important people.
I'm delighted…

177
00:11:58,625 --> 00:12:01,333
If you're late again, you're out.
And your recent performance…

178
00:12:01,416 --> 00:12:03,166
Sorry, we were just rehearsing.

179
00:12:03,250 --> 00:12:05,166
Ms. Robinson hates lateness.

180
00:12:06,125 --> 00:12:06,958
Ms. Robinson?

181
00:12:08,166 --> 00:12:09,500
She'll be at the showcase.

182
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:13,000
-Guys, we're so screwed.
-Now what?

183
00:12:13,083 --> 00:12:15,541
-We really need to go for it.
-Let's go.

184
00:12:17,416 --> 00:12:20,000
Sure, go. It's not like we're in class.

185
00:12:23,458 --> 00:12:26,500
-Are you crazy?
-Ben, you have to come with us now.

186
00:12:26,583 --> 00:12:30,041
-I don't have to do anything.
-Ms. Robinson is coming to the showcase.

187
00:12:30,125 --> 00:12:31,125
So what?

188
00:12:31,208 --> 00:12:34,500
"So what?" This is our chance.
We can't do this without you.

189
00:12:34,583 --> 00:12:37,250
-It's not my problem anymore.
-Yes, it is, Ben.

190
00:12:37,333 --> 00:12:39,666
We built this together. It's about us.

191
00:12:40,625 --> 00:12:43,083
Are you crazy?
You want to throw it all away?

192
00:12:43,166 --> 00:12:45,041
-All of our songs?
-Give it to me.

193
00:12:45,125 --> 00:12:48,333
Just listen to us. What's wrong with you?

194
00:12:48,875 --> 00:12:51,041
I already told you. I'm done.

195
00:12:51,125 --> 00:12:53,916
-You're the reason we even started.
-Ben…

196
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,333
Come on, bro! It's pointless without you.

197
00:13:03,625 --> 00:13:05,833
Ben, running away isn't a solution.

198
00:13:05,916 --> 00:13:07,583
-Wait.
-What the hell is this?

199
00:13:07,666 --> 00:13:11,291
-Without you, we can forget about 5G.
-You're better off without me.

200
00:13:11,375 --> 00:13:13,833
You were just taking a break, right?

201
00:13:13,916 --> 00:13:17,166
A break? This is no longer a break.

202
00:13:21,416 --> 00:13:22,458
Bro…

203
00:13:25,583 --> 00:13:26,416
Okay.

204
00:13:27,791 --> 00:13:29,291
You've convinced me.

205
00:13:29,916 --> 00:13:30,958
-Really?
-No.

206
00:13:50,041 --> 00:13:53,041
Forget it, dude. We're going
to the rehearsal room now, okay?

207
00:13:55,416 --> 00:13:56,708
Cut the crap, okay?

208
00:14:00,791 --> 00:14:02,500
Guys, just chill.

209
00:14:04,083 --> 00:14:04,916
Hey.

210
00:14:07,958 --> 00:14:08,791
Hey.

211
00:14:08,875 --> 00:14:10,458
Seriously, that's enough.

212
00:14:10,541 --> 00:14:14,791
<i>And then more stupid lies</i>

213
00:14:14,875 --> 00:14:16,583
It's the song from this morning.

214
00:14:16,666 --> 00:14:18,791
Everyone's trying to figure out
who sings it.

215
00:14:19,791 --> 00:14:22,458
<i>I still hear you</i>

216
00:14:22,541 --> 00:14:26,750
<i>In the midnight air</i>

217
00:14:29,416 --> 00:14:33,166
Don't you feel it, too?
Let's just continue together.

218
00:14:37,375 --> 00:14:38,250
I can't.

219
00:14:39,541 --> 00:14:43,041
{\an8}<i>And then like ghosts</i>

220
00:14:46,958 --> 00:14:49,500
{\an8}<i>I still hear you</i>

221
00:14:49,583 --> 00:14:53,458
{\an8}<i>In the midnight air</i>

222
00:14:54,958 --> 00:14:58,500
<i>Your voice is the wind</i>
<i>It's the sound</i>

223
00:14:58,583 --> 00:15:02,875
<i>Of a whispered prayer</i>

224
00:15:06,625 --> 00:15:08,791
<i>And then like ghosts</i>

225
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,208
<i>Just a memory</i>

226
00:15:19,291 --> 00:15:20,500
What was that?

227
00:15:22,166 --> 00:15:23,375
Is everyone all right?

228
00:15:47,041 --> 00:15:49,541
Is it me, or does something
look weird here?

229
00:15:52,833 --> 00:15:53,750
Oh, my God, guys.

230
00:15:56,166 --> 00:15:59,916
My phone. It was at 70%,
and now it's not working at all.

231
00:16:01,666 --> 00:16:03,750
-Mine's not working either.
-Same.

232
00:16:04,833 --> 00:16:07,541
-We're in hell.
-How did you come to that conclusion?

233
00:16:07,625 --> 00:16:12,000
-In heaven, my phone would still work.
-Yes, that's a bug.

234
00:16:13,958 --> 00:16:15,500
This can't be happening.

235
00:16:16,125 --> 00:16:19,333
I'll tell you what can't be happening,
that you're all in my way.

236
00:16:21,583 --> 00:16:22,416
Hi.

237
00:16:25,291 --> 00:16:26,208
Hi.

238
00:16:28,541 --> 00:16:30,458
Sorry, I didn't do it on purpose.

239
00:16:30,541 --> 00:16:32,333
-Hey.
-Just ignore them.

240
00:16:32,416 --> 00:16:34,708
They can't control their balls.

241
00:16:34,791 --> 00:16:36,958
It's fine. Everything's okay. All good.

242
00:16:37,541 --> 00:16:41,250
-What are you doing here?
-We're students here.

243
00:16:41,791 --> 00:16:43,833
Really? I've never seen you before.

244
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:46,041
-You either.
-MJ?

245
00:16:46,791 --> 00:16:51,208
-I was looking for you. We'll be late.
-Yeah, okay. Just chill.

246
00:16:51,291 --> 00:16:54,333
-This is…
-Hi. Oli.

247
00:16:54,416 --> 00:16:56,125
-Chris.
-Jonas.

248
00:16:56,208 --> 00:16:57,416
Matteo.

249
00:16:57,500 --> 00:16:58,833
Ben.

250
00:16:59,541 --> 00:17:02,750
-I'm pleased to meet you.
-Well, that makes one of us.

251
00:17:02,833 --> 00:17:05,416
Listen, we have to go.
Science club is about to start.

252
00:17:05,500 --> 00:17:09,208
Okay, just a sec. Did you hear
about the house party tonight?

253
00:17:10,375 --> 00:17:11,750
I'll give you the address.

254
00:17:21,041 --> 00:17:22,708
Yes, so maybe…

255
00:17:23,583 --> 00:17:27,041
-See you later?
-Yes, see you later. Or some other time.

256
00:17:29,458 --> 00:17:32,041
If you keep drooling,
I'll have to get you a bucket.

257
00:17:33,333 --> 00:17:34,500
Guys?

258
00:17:35,083 --> 00:17:35,916
What's up?

259
00:17:39,083 --> 00:17:41,750
-What the hell?
-This has got to be a joke, right?

260
00:17:41,833 --> 00:17:43,458
It has to be a joke.

261
00:17:43,541 --> 00:17:44,833
-This can't be real.
-Shit!

262
00:17:44,916 --> 00:17:45,750
No!

263
00:17:47,291 --> 00:17:49,916
<i>How the hell did we end up in 1995?</i>

264
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,791
-Shit!
-I don't know either! Damn!

265
00:17:52,875 --> 00:17:56,208
-Come on. Get in.
-This is just a bad dream.

266
00:17:56,291 --> 00:17:59,041
Let's go up, then down again.
It'll be back to normal.

267
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:09,000
-Maybe they're hipsters.
-I think it worked.

268
00:18:11,041 --> 00:18:13,083
-No, shit.
-Damn!

269
00:18:13,166 --> 00:18:15,875
Damn, this can't be happening.
Is this a dream or something?

270
00:18:19,750 --> 00:18:21,416
Oh, no!

271
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:23,750
-Again.
-Okay, come on.

272
00:18:24,791 --> 00:18:27,375
-What? No.
-This can't be happening!

273
00:18:28,666 --> 00:18:29,500
This sucks.

274
00:18:30,125 --> 00:18:31,458
No!

275
00:18:36,541 --> 00:18:37,791
Shit!

276
00:18:39,666 --> 00:18:41,333
Let's try it again. Come on.

277
00:18:45,791 --> 00:18:47,250
Come on, guys. Let's go.

278
00:18:50,250 --> 00:18:52,375
Are there any other elevators here?

279
00:18:52,875 --> 00:18:55,875
That can travel through time?

280
00:18:57,375 --> 00:18:59,666
Was that a yes or a no?

281
00:19:05,083 --> 00:19:07,791
"Nothing you hear will show you the way."

282
00:19:08,333 --> 00:19:09,666
"Only what remains will."

283
00:19:10,416 --> 00:19:11,916
"Silent and abundant."

284
00:19:13,125 --> 00:19:15,250
"What sounds devoid of truth

285
00:19:16,166 --> 00:19:17,500
moves nothing forward."

286
00:19:17,916 --> 00:19:19,291
"And nothing will succeed."

287
00:19:20,833 --> 00:19:22,333
Ben? Come on.

288
00:19:26,333 --> 00:19:27,291
Hey!

289
00:19:38,125 --> 00:19:40,708
Guys, how do we get back to our time?

290
00:19:41,333 --> 00:19:42,458
No idea.

291
00:19:45,916 --> 00:19:47,458
Wait, is that a telephone?

292
00:19:48,333 --> 00:19:49,666
It's a telephone, right?

293
00:19:49,750 --> 00:19:52,500
Maybe we can call someone.
Do you remember any phone numbers?

294
00:19:52,583 --> 00:19:54,208
-No.
-I don't know any, either.

295
00:19:56,416 --> 00:19:57,750
I only know 911.

296
00:19:57,833 --> 00:19:59,166
-No.
-Okay.

297
00:20:00,791 --> 00:20:02,000
What's this?

298
00:20:03,416 --> 00:20:05,791
-The instruction manual.
-No, that can't be it.

299
00:20:05,875 --> 00:20:08,125
Those are names
and those are phone numbers.

300
00:20:08,208 --> 00:20:10,625
-What? Just like that in public?
-Yes. Look.

301
00:20:10,708 --> 00:20:11,916
What about privacy laws?

302
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,833
Check if my grandma is in there.
We could call her.

303
00:20:14,916 --> 00:20:16,625
Great idea. And what will you tell her?

304
00:20:16,708 --> 00:20:18,750
"Hey, I'm your grandchild
from the future."

305
00:20:18,833 --> 00:20:22,833
"I'd like to go back.
I'm on campus. Can you pick me up?"

306
00:20:22,916 --> 00:20:25,458
Yes, exactly. <i>Back to the Future.</i>

307
00:20:25,541 --> 00:20:27,833
What if it's just like that movie?

308
00:20:27,916 --> 00:20:29,750
There was this crazy scientist.

309
00:20:29,833 --> 00:20:32,625
That girl earlier was wearing
a science club T-shirt.

310
00:20:32,708 --> 00:20:34,666
Her friend flirted with Ben.

311
00:20:34,750 --> 00:20:36,375
She didn't flirt with me.

312
00:20:36,458 --> 00:20:38,583
-Did she?
-She must know a professor

313
00:20:38,666 --> 00:20:40,375
who can help us to get back home.

314
00:20:40,458 --> 00:20:43,625
You're saying
that we need to find a professor

315
00:20:43,708 --> 00:20:46,583
who is building a time machine
out of a DeLorean

316
00:20:46,666 --> 00:20:48,666
that only works if lightning strikes

317
00:20:48,750 --> 00:20:52,916
the town hall clock at 10:04 p.m.
and powers the DeLorean

318
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,541
with 1.21 gigawatts via the power line,

319
00:20:55,625 --> 00:20:58,333
at exactly 88 miles per hour, by the way,

320
00:20:58,416 --> 00:21:00,916
so that we travel through time
and get back to the future

321
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,166
like Michael J. Fox did
in his iconic role?

322
00:21:05,333 --> 00:21:06,208
Yes.

323
00:21:07,166 --> 00:21:08,000
Okay.

324
00:21:09,333 --> 00:21:11,958
Hey, Chris. Isn't this is your address?

325
00:21:25,125 --> 00:21:27,625
Looks like a decent party.

326
00:21:32,666 --> 00:21:33,833
Are you stupid?

327
00:21:34,625 --> 00:21:35,583
What does he want?

328
00:21:38,541 --> 00:21:41,583
-What a jerk.
-Okay, come on. Let's go.

329
00:21:56,208 --> 00:21:58,958
-What should I do if I see my dad?
-That's right.

330
00:21:59,041 --> 00:22:03,125
As we know from <i>Back to the Future,</i>
we mustn't interfere with the past.

331
00:22:03,208 --> 00:22:05,208
So, when you see your dad,
don't talk to him.

332
00:22:05,291 --> 00:22:07,458
What if he recognizes me?

333
00:22:07,541 --> 00:22:10,708
-You haven't even been born yet.
-Oh, yeah, that's right.

334
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:15,791
As long as we don't prevent our parents
from meeting, it should be okay, right?

335
00:22:15,875 --> 00:22:17,375
Jonas is absolutely right.

336
00:22:17,458 --> 00:22:20,833
Everything we do here
can change the space-time continuum.

337
00:22:20,916 --> 00:22:21,750
Nice camera.

338
00:22:21,833 --> 00:22:23,708
There could be dire consequences.

339
00:22:23,791 --> 00:22:25,750
-"Space-time continuum"?
-Yes.

340
00:22:25,833 --> 00:22:27,416
-What consequences?
-No idea.

341
00:22:27,500 --> 00:22:30,458
This might come as a surprise,
but I've never time traveled before.

342
00:22:30,541 --> 00:22:32,500
-Yes, okay. Fair.
-What is he doing?

343
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:39,333
-So, you can't swipe?
-Well, you swiped it from me.

344
00:22:39,416 --> 00:22:43,583
My father won't even let us rehearse here,
and he does stuff like this.

345
00:22:43,666 --> 00:22:45,708
-Unbelievable.
-Oli, come on.

346
00:22:45,791 --> 00:22:47,833
Sorry, he talks first and thinks later.

347
00:22:47,916 --> 00:22:51,416
Okay. Just let me know
when the thinking has started.

348
00:22:52,083 --> 00:22:55,916
Okay, one more time for everyone.
If we want to get back, remember this.

349
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:59,416
We don't get involved with anyone
and remain inconspicuous.

350
00:22:59,500 --> 00:23:02,875
-About getting "involved"…
-Don't make out with anyone, Oli.

351
00:23:02,958 --> 00:23:04,500
-Hey, guys.
-I was just asking.

352
00:23:04,583 --> 00:23:08,583
-There's the girl from the science club.
-Is nobody listening to me?

353
00:23:09,875 --> 00:23:11,958
Hey, Jamie, MJ, come over here.

354
00:23:12,041 --> 00:23:14,166
Next round, you two against the two of us.

355
00:23:14,250 --> 00:23:18,333
And if you win,
I'll lend you <i>Tekken</i> for a month?

356
00:23:18,416 --> 00:23:20,083
I already made a copy of <i>Tekken.</i>

357
00:23:20,166 --> 00:23:22,583
We'd rather have your time slot
for the exam tomorrow.

358
00:23:23,583 --> 00:23:24,750
No way.

359
00:23:24,833 --> 00:23:27,125
Looks like someone's scared. Right, MJ?

360
00:23:27,208 --> 00:23:29,291
-Yes!
-What do we get if we win?

361
00:23:29,791 --> 00:23:32,833
You'll win Mary Jane's
exclusive Veruca Salt T-shirt.

362
00:23:32,916 --> 00:23:35,375
-Absolutely not.
-Only if she takes it off right here.

363
00:23:35,458 --> 00:23:37,208
-Okay, deal.
-Deal? Are you crazy?

364
00:23:37,291 --> 00:23:38,791
-Hey.
-He won't win anyway.

365
00:23:38,875 --> 00:23:40,541
Oh, you made it.

366
00:23:40,625 --> 00:23:43,041
Even without Google Maps.

367
00:23:44,916 --> 00:23:46,166
Without a map.

368
00:23:46,250 --> 00:23:48,750
You're in the science club, right?

369
00:23:49,583 --> 00:23:52,750
-What do you want?
-I just have a quick question.

370
00:23:52,833 --> 00:23:53,833
Ten seconds.

371
00:23:53,916 --> 00:23:57,958
Do you have a professor
who has a passion for…

372
00:23:58,541 --> 00:23:59,791
Time travel?

373
00:24:01,958 --> 00:24:05,208
<i>And the cat's in the cradle</i>
<i>and the silver spoon</i>

374
00:24:05,708 --> 00:24:08,291
<i>Little boy blue and the man in the moon</i>

375
00:24:08,708 --> 00:24:11,875
<i>"When you coming home, Dad?"</i>
<i>"I don't know when"</i>

376
00:24:11,958 --> 00:24:14,625
<i>"We'll have a good time then"</i>

377
00:24:15,333 --> 00:24:17,833
<i>"You know we'll be together then"</i>

378
00:24:19,333 --> 00:24:22,291
-That was really great, Arno.
-Yeah, dude.

379
00:24:26,083 --> 00:24:26,958
Do I know you?

380
00:24:27,708 --> 00:24:31,291
-Theoretically, it's possible, but…
-Not tonight.

381
00:24:31,375 --> 00:24:33,791
-We need beer.
-I'll get it. Can you hold my bag?

382
00:24:33,875 --> 00:24:37,125
-Yes, sure.
-And, Chad? You've lost something.

383
00:24:37,208 --> 00:24:38,666
Loser.

384
00:24:38,750 --> 00:24:40,750
-Loser!
-Oh, wow.

385
00:24:40,833 --> 00:24:43,541
Why doesn't she study music,
if she can sing that well?

386
00:24:43,625 --> 00:24:45,583
You shouldn't study it at all.

387
00:24:51,500 --> 00:24:53,166
Is there anything besides beer?

388
00:24:53,250 --> 00:24:55,125
What would the gentleman like then?

389
00:24:57,375 --> 00:24:58,250
Sex on the Beach?

390
00:25:00,250 --> 00:25:02,416
At least invite me to dinner first.

391
00:25:06,416 --> 00:25:07,375
I…

392
00:25:08,291 --> 00:25:09,791
You play the guitar?

393
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:12,333
-Crazy.
-I play the guitar.

394
00:25:13,583 --> 00:25:14,500
Yes.

395
00:25:15,458 --> 00:25:17,666
-Do you play the guitar as well?
-Yes.

396
00:25:17,750 --> 00:25:20,375
If you'd like,
we could jam together sometime.

397
00:25:20,458 --> 00:25:22,500
Yes, sure. That would be great.

398
00:25:22,583 --> 00:25:25,458
Right now… It's just not the right time.

399
00:25:25,541 --> 00:25:27,458
-Sorry.
-Totally. Sure.

400
00:25:27,958 --> 00:25:29,708
-I totally understand.
-Cool.

401
00:25:31,541 --> 00:25:33,958
Your cocktail is ready.
You just need a straw.

402
00:25:34,041 --> 00:25:35,291
They're in this drawer.

403
00:25:41,791 --> 00:25:44,916
Oh, shit, what happened? Let me see.

404
00:25:45,541 --> 00:25:48,541
-You need a band-aid.
-It's fine. Chris can take care of it.

405
00:25:48,625 --> 00:25:50,625
There's a first-aid kit upstairs.

406
00:25:51,750 --> 00:25:54,125
Who's Chris? How… do you know that?

407
00:25:55,458 --> 00:25:57,750
There's always a first-aid kit upstairs.

408
00:26:02,208 --> 00:26:03,625
Okay, let's go upstairs.

409
00:26:04,833 --> 00:26:05,875
Okay.

410
00:26:06,500 --> 00:26:07,958
Thanks, MJ.

411
00:26:11,458 --> 00:26:12,958
Don't forget to breathe.

412
00:26:29,375 --> 00:26:31,000
The station is on 95.6.

413
00:26:31,583 --> 00:26:33,541
Bullshit. It's always been on 95.9.

414
00:26:41,208 --> 00:26:43,041
It's <i>Submission Hour</i>.

415
00:26:45,500 --> 00:26:47,625
Sorry. Hey, what's going on?

416
00:26:49,125 --> 00:26:51,500
-It's <i>Submission Hour</i>?
-What's that?

417
00:26:51,875 --> 00:26:55,625
Are you serious? Campus Radio plays songs
submitted by students once a week.

418
00:26:55,708 --> 00:26:57,541
It's always so cool.

419
00:26:58,958 --> 00:27:04,125
<i>Hi, guys. Welcome to</i> Submission Hour.
<i>Today we're playing…</i>

420
00:27:04,208 --> 00:27:07,708
-Kira, hi. Cool name.
-Thanks. Nice to meet you.

421
00:27:07,791 --> 00:27:09,416
-Daniel?
-Yes?

422
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:11,000
Let him take a hit, too.

423
00:27:11,583 --> 00:27:12,416
Sure.

424
00:27:17,750 --> 00:27:18,583
Daniel Weber?

425
00:27:20,333 --> 00:27:21,166
Yes?

426
00:27:27,625 --> 00:27:29,166
I think you'll survive.

427
00:27:30,500 --> 00:27:33,500
That's a shame. I was already expecting
to make an epic exit.

428
00:27:33,583 --> 00:27:35,458
Yes. I'm sorry about that.

429
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:37,625
No heroic death for you.

430
00:27:38,875 --> 00:27:40,458
Are you studying medicine?

431
00:27:41,250 --> 00:27:46,041
Or… do you have a secret double life
as a campus paramedic?

432
00:27:46,125 --> 00:27:49,083
I'm studying computer science.

433
00:27:50,166 --> 00:27:52,375
-And you?
-It's complicated.

434
00:27:53,666 --> 00:27:54,500
Okay.

435
00:27:57,208 --> 00:27:58,791
What's "Veruca Salt"?

436
00:28:00,541 --> 00:28:03,833
Are you serious?
It's just the best band ever.

437
00:28:03,916 --> 00:28:06,708
-That nobody knows?
-It doesn't make them any less good.

438
00:28:06,791 --> 00:28:08,666
It's alternative rock.

439
00:28:09,250 --> 00:28:11,375
<i>American Thighs</i> is a masterpiece.

440
00:28:11,958 --> 00:28:13,250
I prefer pop music.

441
00:28:13,333 --> 00:28:17,041
-Like New Kids On The Block?
-Don't trash pop music, okay?

442
00:28:17,125 --> 00:28:19,875
I like it more complex.
Pop always has the same four chords.

443
00:28:19,958 --> 00:28:22,791
C major, G major, A minor, and F major.
Just not that great.

444
00:28:22,875 --> 00:28:24,500
-Right?
-You can't be serious.

445
00:28:24,583 --> 00:28:27,166
"God Only Knows" has
an amazing chord progression.

446
00:28:27,250 --> 00:28:30,500
And why does a computer science student
know so much about chords?

447
00:28:30,583 --> 00:28:32,958
Why does a 20-year-old
listen to The Beach Boys?

448
00:28:33,041 --> 00:28:35,458
A 20-year-old
who has a clue about good music.

449
00:28:37,541 --> 00:28:40,041
And what exactly makes music good for you?

450
00:28:40,125 --> 00:28:41,666
Well, when it's just perfect.

451
00:28:42,333 --> 00:28:44,041
When everything is in the right place.

452
00:28:45,541 --> 00:28:46,375
And for you?

453
00:28:48,041 --> 00:28:51,125
When it gets to you
before you can think about it.

454
00:29:07,208 --> 00:29:08,416
Jamie?

455
00:29:12,083 --> 00:29:13,208
Take it off.

456
00:29:14,125 --> 00:29:15,458
Not you, her!

457
00:29:15,958 --> 00:29:17,833
-MJ, you have to take your clothes off.
-What?

458
00:29:17,916 --> 00:29:21,625
Yes, your T-shirt. The Veruca Salt shirt.

459
00:29:22,250 --> 00:29:23,916
-A deal's a deal.
-Shit.

460
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,750
I totally forgot about the game.
Jamie, I'm so sorry.

461
00:29:26,833 --> 00:29:30,291
I'm sorry. I didn't win, but I…

462
00:29:30,375 --> 00:29:34,583
-You just need to take the shirt off…
-No.

463
00:29:36,041 --> 00:29:37,208
Jamie, are you okay?

464
00:29:41,125 --> 00:29:43,500
-Maybe I'll see you tomorrow?
-I don't know yet.

465
00:29:44,041 --> 00:29:45,666
Are you leaving tomorrow?

466
00:29:46,291 --> 00:29:47,708
I don't know that either.

467
00:29:48,291 --> 00:29:49,541
Dude!

468
00:29:49,625 --> 00:29:52,041
Okay, whatever.
I have to take care of my friend.

469
00:29:54,791 --> 00:29:59,333
We also need to…
Because of the exam tomorrow. The thingy.

470
00:30:00,916 --> 00:30:02,000
-Bye, Chad.
-Yes.

471
00:30:02,083 --> 00:30:03,333
-Ciao.
-Bye.

472
00:30:07,291 --> 00:30:08,458
It's all good, big guy.

473
00:30:11,416 --> 00:30:12,458
And down we go!

474
00:30:13,083 --> 00:30:17,625
<i>Okay, guys. This next one</i>
<i>will blow you away. Listen up…</i>

475
00:30:17,708 --> 00:30:19,708
You can't ever take an elevator again.

476
00:30:23,791 --> 00:30:26,166
-Of course.
-I'm serious.

477
00:30:26,250 --> 00:30:29,125
<i>And then like ghosts</i>

478
00:30:29,208 --> 00:30:32,833
<i>Just a memory</i>

479
00:30:34,958 --> 00:30:38,000
<i>Drift through the night, through the dark</i>

480
00:30:38,083 --> 00:30:41,166
<i>Where we used to be</i>

481
00:30:45,125 --> 00:30:48,250
<i>Dust and coffee rings…</i>

482
00:30:48,333 --> 00:30:50,083
It's the song from the elevator.

483
00:30:50,166 --> 00:30:53,208
<i>On the windowsill</i>

484
00:30:55,916 --> 00:30:58,875
<i>Proof of the nights</i>

485
00:30:58,958 --> 00:31:03,000
<i>We used to sit there still</i>

486
00:31:06,083 --> 00:31:08,750
Do you know the title of this song?
And who sings it?

487
00:31:09,791 --> 00:31:11,291
-No?
-No idea.

488
00:31:16,250 --> 00:31:17,208
Hey, guys!

489
00:31:18,375 --> 00:31:19,291
Hey, there's Oli.

490
00:31:20,250 --> 00:31:21,916
I think he wants us to join him.

491
00:31:22,458 --> 00:31:23,958
Brilliant deduction.

492
00:31:24,041 --> 00:31:26,000
-Come here!
-Oli, don't be an ass.

493
00:31:26,875 --> 00:31:29,583
<i>I still hear you</i>

494
00:31:29,666 --> 00:31:32,833
<i>In the midnight air</i>

495
00:31:32,916 --> 00:31:33,916
Well?

496
00:31:35,916 --> 00:31:37,083
Don't you recognize it?

497
00:31:37,166 --> 00:31:39,083
-It's the song from the elevator.
-Exactly.

498
00:31:39,166 --> 00:31:41,750
I was playing it
when the time travel happened.

499
00:31:41,833 --> 00:31:43,750
Guys, it can't be a coincidence.

500
00:31:43,833 --> 00:31:47,708
You think we ended up here
because of the song? That's impossible.

501
00:31:47,791 --> 00:31:49,958
Okay, and you're a time travel expert now?

502
00:31:50,041 --> 00:31:53,625
-No, but it just can't be.
-In most time travel movies,

503
00:31:53,708 --> 00:31:56,500
you must exactly recreate
what you did to get back home.

504
00:31:56,583 --> 00:32:01,750
Basically, we need to find this song.
Campus Radio must have a copy of it.

505
00:32:01,833 --> 00:32:04,666
<i>And then we'll go into the elevator</i>
<i>and play the song…</i>

506
00:32:05,791 --> 00:32:07,541
<i>…like we did before.</i>

507
00:32:07,625 --> 00:32:10,291
<i>And that will take us back to the future.</i>

508
00:32:12,375 --> 00:32:16,041
That's nonsense.
I think this is about something else.

509
00:32:16,500 --> 00:32:20,083
I think we're here to convince my dad
to let me continue with music.

510
00:32:22,166 --> 00:32:25,041
Who's in favor of finding the song
and going back to the elevator?

511
00:32:25,583 --> 00:32:26,708
-Yes.
-Yes.

512
00:32:27,291 --> 00:32:30,583
-Let me do some more research.
-Nothing else really makes any sense.

513
00:32:31,916 --> 00:32:35,750
-Where is Oli going?
-What's up? All good?

514
00:32:37,208 --> 00:32:39,416
Look at how much fun he's having.

515
00:32:39,500 --> 00:32:43,041
This our only chance ever
to experience a real '90s party.

516
00:32:43,125 --> 00:32:44,541
Let's just have a good time.

517
00:32:45,083 --> 00:32:46,458
-Yes, but…
-Hey, bro.

518
00:32:46,958 --> 00:32:50,000
Chris is right.
Just think of it… as research.

519
00:34:42,458 --> 00:34:44,000
Let's get some breakfast.

520
00:34:48,250 --> 00:34:49,416
I'll check the kitchen.

521
00:34:53,583 --> 00:34:54,916
I don't know what's worse.

522
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:59,791
My hangover, or the fact
that we're still stuck in 1995.

523
00:34:59,875 --> 00:35:02,625
Guys, you'll never guess
what happened to me yesterday.

524
00:35:02,708 --> 00:35:05,041
I tried to save Daniel Weber.

525
00:35:05,125 --> 00:35:07,166
-Who?
-The guy from the plaque?

526
00:35:08,125 --> 00:35:09,083
No elevators, okay?

527
00:35:09,166 --> 00:35:12,875
-Wait, when did the elevator crash again?
-It's on the plaque.

528
00:35:16,083 --> 00:35:17,791
On June 6th, 1995.

529
00:35:19,083 --> 00:35:21,916
-And what's today's date?
-June 6th.

530
00:35:23,500 --> 00:35:25,208
The elevator was destroyed in the crash.

531
00:35:25,291 --> 00:35:28,083
That means if we can't get back
before the accident…

532
00:35:28,166 --> 00:35:30,416
-Then we'll be stuck here forever.
-Shit.

533
00:35:31,916 --> 00:35:33,166
What time did it crash?

534
00:35:33,791 --> 00:35:37,166
-After the last lecture.
-So, around 6:00 p.m.?

535
00:35:37,875 --> 00:35:40,375
That means we have eight hours
to find the song.

536
00:35:40,458 --> 00:35:43,833
-How do you know the time?
-I borrowed a watch from Chris's house.

537
00:35:43,916 --> 00:35:45,416
-Borrowed?
-Yes.

538
00:35:45,500 --> 00:35:46,875
Well, guys…

539
00:35:48,250 --> 00:35:50,791
-Where do we start?
-I have an idea.

540
00:35:53,250 --> 00:35:54,333
-Hi.
-Hello.

541
00:35:55,250 --> 00:35:56,750
Hey! How are you?

542
00:35:57,666 --> 00:36:01,375
-Have you come to steal my camera again?
-I gave it back to you.

543
00:36:03,958 --> 00:36:07,541
Could you tell us where we can find
the Campus Radio host?

544
00:36:08,583 --> 00:36:10,666
-We're big fans.
-Huge fans.

545
00:36:10,750 --> 00:36:12,041
-Yes.
-Yes.

546
00:36:14,166 --> 00:36:16,875
Crazy sound, right? Jesus Christ!

547
00:36:19,541 --> 00:36:21,958
This is not a song, it's a wrecking ball.

548
00:36:22,041 --> 00:36:24,666
It's as if Blondie
and The Clash had a baby.

549
00:36:29,333 --> 00:36:31,166
Come on!

550
00:36:31,250 --> 00:36:32,166
Damn it.

551
00:36:35,750 --> 00:36:37,166
What a load of crap.

552
00:36:43,833 --> 00:36:46,875
Sorry for the interruption.
As always, everything is fine.

553
00:36:46,958 --> 00:36:49,291
My name is Marty
and you're listening to <i>Heart Beat.</i>

554
00:36:50,791 --> 00:36:53,208
-Hi.
-Oh, no, guys. Seriously?

555
00:36:53,291 --> 00:36:55,708
Olivia, when I said
I needed some new interns,

556
00:36:55,791 --> 00:36:59,125
I didn't mean boys.
When will we get some girls in here?

557
00:36:59,208 --> 00:37:01,500
-Nothing personal, Olivia.
-It's so weird.

558
00:37:01,583 --> 00:37:04,208
Why don't young women want to work
with a 40-year-old loser

559
00:37:04,291 --> 00:37:06,291
who's still around
ten years after graduating?

560
00:37:07,291 --> 00:37:08,791
-Hey!
-You're a creep.

561
00:37:08,875 --> 00:37:10,500
I'm not a creep.

562
00:37:10,583 --> 00:37:14,166
-We're just here about a song…
-Your name is Olivia?

563
00:37:14,250 --> 00:37:17,250
-Do you want to know my name?
-No, I don't.

564
00:37:17,333 --> 00:37:19,333
-How did the song go?
-It was like…

565
00:37:24,416 --> 00:37:26,500
Yes, that song was the bomb.

566
00:37:26,583 --> 00:37:29,208
Exactly. We need it please.
Do you have the tape?

567
00:37:29,750 --> 00:37:30,833
That's not possible.

568
00:37:30,916 --> 00:37:32,750
-Why?
-It was stolen.

569
00:37:32,833 --> 00:37:35,916
Breakup song. They must have regretted
submitting it. Happens a lot.

570
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:40,041
-But, hey, that's a great song.
-Stolen? Who stole it?

571
00:37:40,125 --> 00:37:42,666
-Do you have cameras here?
-Cameras? Yes, of course.

572
00:37:43,166 --> 00:37:45,083
Everywhere. Can't you see them?

573
00:37:45,625 --> 00:37:49,083
The forensic team is currently
analyzing the fingerprints.

574
00:37:49,166 --> 00:37:51,625
There must be a list showing
who comes in and out.

575
00:37:53,333 --> 00:37:55,250
Listen, who are you guys anyway?

576
00:37:55,916 --> 00:37:59,041
-A film crew.
-Yes, we're filming a documentary.

577
00:37:59,125 --> 00:38:00,916
We're looking for new music stars.

578
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:04,583
-A documentary team without a camera?
-We're still doing research.

579
00:38:04,666 --> 00:38:10,291
-Can you tell us who submitted it?
-There was something on the tape, but…

580
00:38:10,875 --> 00:38:13,750
No idea, I can't remember.
Can you close the door on your way out?

581
00:38:13,833 --> 00:38:14,875
Shit.

582
00:38:15,541 --> 00:38:16,541
Yes, okay.

583
00:38:17,125 --> 00:38:18,250
Thanks.

584
00:38:20,625 --> 00:38:22,958
I just recalled
what was written on the tape.

585
00:38:23,041 --> 00:38:24,041
"Charlie!"

586
00:38:24,125 --> 00:38:26,958
Yes, that's it. "Charlie C."

587
00:38:27,666 --> 00:38:29,083
-"Charlie C."
-Okay. Cool.

588
00:38:29,166 --> 00:38:30,125
-Thanks.
-Thanks.

589
00:38:30,833 --> 00:38:33,541
Okay, Oli. You get Charlie C.'s file.

590
00:38:33,625 --> 00:38:36,166
-Then we'll know who wrote the song.
-Okay.

591
00:38:36,250 --> 00:38:38,208
I'll send it to you. Yes.

592
00:38:39,041 --> 00:38:40,000
-Bye.
<i>-Bye.</i>

593
00:38:41,333 --> 00:38:44,375
I'm here to pick up my transcript.

594
00:38:44,916 --> 00:38:46,583
-Name?
-Charlie.

595
00:38:47,541 --> 00:38:48,875
And the last name?

596
00:38:48,958 --> 00:38:50,458
Charlie C.

597
00:38:50,541 --> 00:38:51,541
Do it.

598
00:38:51,666 --> 00:38:55,625
-Hello?
-Charlie C. Well, my last name…

599
00:38:56,375 --> 00:38:57,583
Here we go, buddy.

600
00:38:59,083 --> 00:39:01,875
Got another one with no student ID.
Call his parents.

601
00:39:01,958 --> 00:39:04,375
-As I said, I don't go here.
-Yeah, exactly.

602
00:39:04,458 --> 00:39:07,625
Do you know who I am?
I'm the head of security.

603
00:39:07,708 --> 00:39:09,166
I own this place.

604
00:39:09,250 --> 00:39:13,000
-I just wanted to…
-Don't move. Stay quiet. Don't even blink.

605
00:39:13,666 --> 00:39:15,166
We have to stop.

606
00:39:15,250 --> 00:39:16,458
Call the police.

607
00:39:16,541 --> 00:39:18,250
Shouldn't I call his parents first--

608
00:39:18,333 --> 00:39:19,333
The police!

609
00:39:20,500 --> 00:39:21,500
Sorry.

610
00:39:21,583 --> 00:39:22,458
Let's go.

611
00:39:23,666 --> 00:39:24,500
Hello?

612
00:39:24,583 --> 00:39:27,166
-Let's try the library first.
-Yes, totally.

613
00:39:27,250 --> 00:39:31,125
Let's look through the student register.
Ben, Chris, you check the yearbooks.

614
00:39:31,208 --> 00:39:32,041
-Sure.
-Okay.

615
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:38,791
Crazy, just look at all those photos.
My aunt must be in here.

616
00:39:38,875 --> 00:39:41,500
-With super thin eyebrows.
-For sure.

617
00:39:43,666 --> 00:39:45,250
Look, it's my dad.

618
00:39:46,791 --> 00:39:50,166
Crazy. How did it go?
Did you speak to him?

619
00:39:51,916 --> 00:39:55,375
It was really weird.
I know he's my dad, but…

620
00:39:55,916 --> 00:39:58,625
-But it's like I don't even know him.
-Yes.

621
00:39:58,708 --> 00:40:01,333
And Jonas has been stressing
the whole time.

622
00:40:02,375 --> 00:40:05,208
My parents didn't even meet here
at Heartville, you know?

623
00:40:11,125 --> 00:40:12,708
I think you should talk to him.

624
00:40:14,375 --> 00:40:16,000
You won't get this chance again.

625
00:40:16,583 --> 00:40:19,916
If we don't talk to anyone,
it will look suspicious.

626
00:40:22,125 --> 00:40:22,958
Yes, it…

627
00:40:23,625 --> 00:40:26,708
-It would be a waste not to talk to him.
-Exactly.

628
00:40:26,791 --> 00:40:28,583
-I'll never be here again.
-Yes.

629
00:40:33,958 --> 00:40:36,958
It would be a shame
to not get to know her.

630
00:40:38,041 --> 00:40:38,875
"Her"?

631
00:40:38,958 --> 00:40:40,250
Him.

632
00:40:42,541 --> 00:40:44,416
Well… I heard "her."

633
00:40:45,375 --> 00:40:46,208
No.

634
00:40:48,166 --> 00:40:49,458
No. Him.

635
00:40:49,541 --> 00:40:51,666
Do we even know if Charlie
is a man or a woman?

636
00:40:51,750 --> 00:40:56,541
No idea. Have you noticed how all students
here look like they're in their thirties?

637
00:40:56,625 --> 00:41:01,375
It's the look. Hairstyles, outfits.
In our time, old people look like that.

638
00:41:01,458 --> 00:41:04,875
My dad still has the same haircut
from when he got his license.

639
00:41:04,958 --> 00:41:08,375
My dad wears a fanny pack unironically.

640
00:41:08,458 --> 00:41:10,291
Hey, guys. What's that sound?

641
00:41:11,750 --> 00:41:14,583
It sounds like someone is torturing Siri.

642
00:41:17,750 --> 00:41:20,083
Hey, crazy. They already have computers.

643
00:41:26,416 --> 00:41:27,916
Double-click it.

644
00:41:31,250 --> 00:41:33,375
-Guys, look here.
-Okay.

645
00:41:34,458 --> 00:41:37,250
-Dude!
-Okay. Let's get online.

646
00:41:37,958 --> 00:41:39,708
-Yes, Google, right?
-Yes.

647
00:41:49,750 --> 00:41:53,541
-What?
-Excuse me? It's my turn.

648
00:41:54,166 --> 00:41:57,791
Just a moment. There must be
a database with names.

649
00:41:57,875 --> 00:42:00,333
-On the university server.
-Did you reserve a slot?

650
00:42:00,875 --> 00:42:04,000
-Three minutes. Really.
-Go to the "My Computer" icon.

651
00:42:04,083 --> 00:42:06,083
Did you reserve a slot?

652
00:42:06,166 --> 00:42:07,916
Okay, okay. No need to be so loud.

653
00:42:09,083 --> 00:42:12,375
{\an8}Guys, we'll do it without the Internet.
Here's a Charlie Cane.

654
00:42:12,458 --> 00:42:13,958
{\an8}Here's a Charlie Conrady,

655
00:42:14,041 --> 00:42:16,708
{\an8}Charlie Cress, Charlie Casas.
It could be any of them.

656
00:42:16,791 --> 00:42:19,916
We've got nine names.
One of them must be Charlie C.

657
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:21,875
Not much time, but let's be inconspicuous.

658
00:42:21,958 --> 00:42:26,041
Why do we always build brilliant
things that we can barely carry?

659
00:42:26,125 --> 00:42:29,041
-My arms are killing me right now.
-Yeah, mine, too.

660
00:42:29,750 --> 00:42:32,500
Damn, I left the copies
of the presentation in the library.

661
00:42:34,458 --> 00:42:38,375
Maybe the creep from the radio was right,
and we do need a camera,

662
00:42:38,458 --> 00:42:41,708
-so we can pass as a film crew.
-Why not ask for a red carpet?

663
00:42:41,791 --> 00:42:44,416
-Totally inconspicuous.
-That's not a bad idea.

664
00:42:44,500 --> 00:42:46,666
That's what I'm saying. I'll get us one.

665
00:42:47,208 --> 00:42:48,041
Oh, sorry.

666
00:42:49,166 --> 00:42:50,208
Hey, Ben.

667
00:42:50,875 --> 00:42:51,833
Hi, Mary Jane.

668
00:42:53,041 --> 00:42:54,875
-Do you need help?
-Yes! Could you?

669
00:42:54,958 --> 00:42:55,791
Sure.

670
00:42:56,416 --> 00:42:57,375
Careful.

671
00:42:57,916 --> 00:43:02,208
You go to the seminar room.
I'll get the copies from the library.

672
00:43:02,291 --> 00:43:03,125
Okay.

673
00:43:04,833 --> 00:43:06,208
-Sorry.
-What are you doing?

674
00:43:06,291 --> 00:43:07,541
I'll be back in ten minutes.

675
00:43:09,333 --> 00:43:11,208
He knows that time is running out, right?

676
00:43:23,625 --> 00:43:26,750
-What are they doing here?
-They're ahead of us, unfortunately.

677
00:43:26,833 --> 00:43:29,541
Because you distracted me
from playing beer pong yesterday.

678
00:43:29,625 --> 00:43:31,125
-And that's bad?
-Yes.

679
00:43:31,958 --> 00:43:33,000
It is.

680
00:43:33,083 --> 00:43:36,708
Since our presentation
is now right after lunch.

681
00:43:37,375 --> 00:43:38,208
And?

682
00:43:40,041 --> 00:43:42,125
Well, Jamie says…

683
00:43:43,250 --> 00:43:46,791
the jury will be digesting their food
and will be tired.

684
00:43:47,958 --> 00:43:49,416
-That does suck.
-Yes.

685
00:43:49,500 --> 00:43:50,375
Sure.

686
00:43:51,125 --> 00:43:53,250
-Can I make it up to you somehow.
-Can you?

687
00:43:53,333 --> 00:43:56,041
-I could buy you lunch.
-So I'll be tired as well?

688
00:43:56,875 --> 00:43:59,291
You can't study
on an empty stomach, right?

689
00:43:59,625 --> 00:44:00,500
2:00?

690
00:44:01,916 --> 00:44:04,416
-Okay.
-Ben? Could you leave now?

691
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:06,500
We need to work.

692
00:44:07,083 --> 00:44:09,708
Hi, Jamie. Nice to see you, too.

693
00:44:10,625 --> 00:44:11,875
-Now.
-Yes.

694
00:44:13,291 --> 00:44:14,125
Sorry.

695
00:44:15,416 --> 00:44:16,291
Right.

696
00:44:16,375 --> 00:44:19,750
Here are the copies of the presentation.

697
00:44:19,833 --> 00:44:23,500
And then there was something else
I wanted to discuss with you…

698
00:44:25,291 --> 00:44:31,541
It's right here on page two.

699
00:44:35,375 --> 00:44:38,333
-So, here…
-Yes? Keep talking.

700
00:44:39,500 --> 00:44:41,708
-With…
-What?

701
00:44:41,791 --> 00:44:42,791
And here, look.

702
00:44:54,916 --> 00:44:55,875
Can I help you?

703
00:44:56,500 --> 00:45:00,458
-I didn't see you.
-It's okay. I can't see very well either.

704
00:45:01,041 --> 00:45:03,375
I'm Dolly. How can I help you?

705
00:45:04,375 --> 00:45:07,208
I need a camera,
but I don't know which one.

706
00:45:07,291 --> 00:45:09,125
Are you looking for something specific?

707
00:45:11,208 --> 00:45:12,958
Yes, I'll have a look.

708
00:45:22,791 --> 00:45:27,875
Good choice. This is the first
MiniDV camera in the world.

709
00:45:27,958 --> 00:45:31,500
-Perfect.
-Yes. You'll need something for sound.

710
00:45:31,583 --> 00:45:35,083
This device can record
and play back. Two in one.

711
00:45:35,166 --> 00:45:36,750
Crazy. That's right.

712
00:45:37,500 --> 00:45:41,458
Hi, Olivia. Are you returning the camera?
Did you take nice photos?

713
00:45:41,541 --> 00:45:42,458
Everything okay?

714
00:45:43,625 --> 00:45:44,458
Good.

715
00:45:47,083 --> 00:45:49,291
You gave me hard time
over a borrowed camera?

716
00:45:49,375 --> 00:45:52,000
-I thought it was yours.
-It was mine then.

717
00:45:52,083 --> 00:45:52,916
Now…

718
00:45:55,291 --> 00:45:56,500
-Name?
-Oliver.

719
00:45:56,583 --> 00:45:58,125
-Oliver.
-Oliver.

720
00:45:59,666 --> 00:46:02,291
-Department?
-Film studies.

721
00:46:03,083 --> 00:46:05,583
Film studies? I've never seen you there.

722
00:46:05,666 --> 00:46:07,750
-I'm making a documentary.
-Nobody watches them.

723
00:46:07,833 --> 00:46:10,000
I love documentaries.

724
00:46:10,083 --> 00:46:14,666
In the future, everyone will watch
documentaries. Especially real crime.

725
00:46:16,791 --> 00:46:20,791
That sounds weird, but it doesn't
surprise me coming from you.

726
00:46:22,166 --> 00:46:23,958
I just need your student ID,

727
00:46:24,041 --> 00:46:27,541
and then nothing stands
in the way of your Oscar, right?

728
00:46:28,458 --> 00:46:32,625
Yes, Oliver. Where's your student ID?

729
00:46:34,916 --> 00:46:35,750
Well…

730
00:46:36,541 --> 00:46:39,125
Surely I can come by later with it?

731
00:46:39,208 --> 00:46:40,625
Of course, my dear.

732
00:46:42,333 --> 00:46:43,166
So nice.

733
00:46:43,250 --> 00:46:46,833
I don't know if that's allowed,
you rascal.

734
00:46:47,791 --> 00:46:49,416
Okay, then thank you so much.

735
00:46:50,166 --> 00:46:51,000
Ciao.

736
00:46:52,333 --> 00:46:53,416
Bye, Olivia.

737
00:46:54,583 --> 00:46:56,750
Well, he's going to be famous.

738
00:46:58,458 --> 00:47:00,416
Or he'll be put behind bars.

739
00:47:02,041 --> 00:47:07,500
I think this time it was a bit better,
but I say let's do it again.

740
00:47:15,000 --> 00:47:19,083
It's lunchtime.
I think I should maybe get…

741
00:47:19,166 --> 00:47:22,708
Here. I brought lunch,
so we can keep practicing.

742
00:47:22,791 --> 00:47:27,750
Okay. I have the feeling that the argument
for point four should be more concise.

743
00:47:27,833 --> 00:47:31,250
I'll just get something to drink.
My throat is dry.

744
00:47:31,333 --> 00:47:34,708
Especially when representing
qubits and their statuses…

745
00:47:34,791 --> 00:47:36,416
I want something fizzy.

746
00:47:36,500 --> 00:47:40,833
You want to stop now?
But we're really on a roll.

747
00:47:52,291 --> 00:47:54,041
-Come.
-What took you so long?

748
00:47:54,125 --> 00:47:56,708
It wasn't that easy.
Now let's go and find Charlie C.

749
00:47:56,791 --> 00:47:59,458
-Yes. Let's go.
-Do you think it will take long?

750
00:47:59,541 --> 00:48:01,958
It won't take that long.
We only have 4.5 hours left.

751
00:48:02,041 --> 00:48:06,166
-Why are you asking? Got plans?
-No, I was just asking.

752
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:09,791
-Come on. We don't have much time.
-Two pics each.

753
00:48:09,875 --> 00:48:11,791
Good luck finding the song. Ready?

754
00:48:11,875 --> 00:48:12,708
Let's go.

755
00:48:26,458 --> 00:48:27,291
Charlie Casas?

756
00:48:28,208 --> 00:48:29,458
Are you Charlie Cress?

757
00:48:29,541 --> 00:48:31,375
-Are you Charlie Clint?
-Yes.

758
00:48:31,458 --> 00:48:33,375
Did you submit a song to Campus Radio?

759
00:48:33,458 --> 00:48:35,166
No, I don't even make music.

760
00:48:35,250 --> 00:48:36,166
No.

761
00:48:37,208 --> 00:48:38,500
Okay, sorry.

762
00:48:46,208 --> 00:48:48,291
-Charlie Chester?
-Are you Charlie Clark?

763
00:48:48,375 --> 00:48:51,041
Did you submit a song to Campus Radio?

764
00:48:51,583 --> 00:48:53,333
Sounds great. Keep going.

765
00:48:53,416 --> 00:48:54,333
Just one.

766
00:49:11,500 --> 00:49:13,083
-Sure?
-Yes.

767
00:49:13,166 --> 00:49:14,125
Okay.

768
00:49:14,208 --> 00:49:16,458
-Get me higher.
-I'm trying.

769
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:22,125
-No.
-No?

770
00:49:26,000 --> 00:49:28,541
{\an8}-That's our last Charlie C.
-Ladies.

771
00:49:28,625 --> 00:49:29,958
Out of the way, dude.

772
00:49:31,791 --> 00:49:32,916
Charlie Crawford?

773
00:49:33,000 --> 00:49:35,375
Sorry, guys.
I don't sign autographs during training.

774
00:49:35,458 --> 00:49:37,666
-Autograph? Are you nuts?
-Are you serious?

775
00:49:38,166 --> 00:49:40,375
Dude, Charlie's gonna be
an All-Star one day.

776
00:49:41,166 --> 00:49:44,916
-We just have a question.
-He doesn't do interviews. Get lost.

777
00:49:45,000 --> 00:49:47,083
The guy is so clueless, dude.

778
00:49:47,166 --> 00:49:50,416
Hey! We know your song was played
on Campus Radio yesterday.

779
00:49:51,208 --> 00:49:52,375
{\an8}Charlie C.

780
00:49:56,000 --> 00:49:57,166
{\an8}<i>A cassette?</i>

781
00:49:59,833 --> 00:50:00,833
{\an8}<i>What are you doing?</i>

782
00:50:01,958 --> 00:50:03,125
Sorry.

783
00:50:03,833 --> 00:50:07,166
-How did you even find me?
-Your name was on the tape.

784
00:50:08,958 --> 00:50:11,458
-So, what do you want?
-The tape.

785
00:50:13,000 --> 00:50:14,791
Sorry, you've got the wrong guy.

786
00:50:15,875 --> 00:50:17,166
Wait, it isn't your song?

787
00:50:17,666 --> 00:50:21,250
I'm an athlete. As if I'd ever make music.

788
00:50:21,333 --> 00:50:24,583
-Nonsense.
-I get it!

789
00:50:24,666 --> 00:50:27,541
The song is about you.
You're the guy who cheated.

790
00:50:30,041 --> 00:50:31,708
I have a reputation to maintain.

791
00:50:32,625 --> 00:50:33,583
And…

792
00:50:34,500 --> 00:50:37,208
if you're smart,
then you'll forget all about this.

793
00:50:37,583 --> 00:50:38,750
Charlie!

794
00:50:44,041 --> 00:50:47,291
If you tell anyone
that the song is about me…

795
00:50:48,791 --> 00:50:50,000
I'll kick your ass.

796
00:50:55,041 --> 00:50:55,875
Great.

797
00:50:56,666 --> 00:50:57,500
Now what?

798
00:50:58,250 --> 00:51:01,583
We need to find Charlie's ex.
The one who wrote the song about him.

799
00:51:02,083 --> 00:51:04,125
Sorry, guys, I need to use the bathroom.

800
00:51:23,500 --> 00:51:25,458
-Hi.
-Hey.

801
00:51:26,500 --> 00:51:29,250
-Sorry I'm late.
-No, it's fine.

802
00:51:29,333 --> 00:51:31,625
-I got us some lunch.
-Nice.

803
00:51:35,500 --> 00:51:37,833
-Can I have one?
-Yes, sure.

804
00:51:40,291 --> 00:51:41,125
Thanks.

805
00:51:47,291 --> 00:51:49,041
-Is this…
-Where did you get that?

806
00:51:49,833 --> 00:51:53,416
You dropped it earlier.
But your lyrics, they are…

807
00:51:53,500 --> 00:51:54,500
You read them?

808
00:51:56,750 --> 00:51:58,083
Your fault. You were late.

809
00:52:01,875 --> 00:52:03,208
Sorry.

810
00:52:06,708 --> 00:52:09,750
I'm sorry, too,
that I looked through it. And, hey…

811
00:52:10,625 --> 00:52:12,250
I really like your lyrics.

812
00:52:13,375 --> 00:52:14,583
-Really?
-Yes.

813
00:52:14,666 --> 00:52:18,666
Yes, they're really great.
Maybe a little too…

814
00:52:20,500 --> 00:52:22,125
-No, never mind.
-Too complex?

815
00:52:23,458 --> 00:52:24,291
No.

816
00:52:25,291 --> 00:52:26,291
A bit too forced.

817
00:52:29,541 --> 00:52:30,708
I've heard that before.

818
00:52:32,791 --> 00:52:34,791
Sorry. I…

819
00:52:35,708 --> 00:52:37,416
I shouldn't have read them.

820
00:52:38,250 --> 00:52:39,666
-I'm sorry.
-It's okay.

821
00:52:40,875 --> 00:52:43,541
Why are you studying computer science?

822
00:52:44,708 --> 00:52:45,625
Instead of music?

823
00:52:47,583 --> 00:52:50,166
-Why?
-It just seems like…

824
00:52:50,875 --> 00:52:53,458
Like you experience music differently
than everyone else.

825
00:52:55,833 --> 00:52:57,666
No, it's just a hobby.

826
00:52:57,750 --> 00:52:59,166
Just a hobby?

827
00:53:02,291 --> 00:53:03,416
Maybe a bit more.

828
00:53:05,750 --> 00:53:08,125
-And what's your excuse?
-My excuse?

829
00:53:08,208 --> 00:53:09,375
Yes, your excuse.

830
00:53:10,291 --> 00:53:14,875
For years, Jamie and I have had
this plan about computer science and…

831
00:53:15,625 --> 00:53:18,000
all the things we'd like
to do together after college.

832
00:53:18,083 --> 00:53:21,916
I don't even know if that's still my plan.

833
00:53:24,125 --> 00:53:28,625
But… it would feel
like I was letting her down

834
00:53:28,708 --> 00:53:32,125
if I suddenly decided
to do something else.

835
00:53:35,833 --> 00:53:38,166
And what if your plan has changed?

836
00:53:44,958 --> 00:53:46,666
What's your excuse?

837
00:53:47,583 --> 00:53:48,750
My excuse?

838
00:53:51,166 --> 00:53:54,041
I tried it. Music, that is.

839
00:53:54,125 --> 00:53:56,166
It didn't work out the way I imagined.

840
00:53:59,375 --> 00:54:01,458
<i>The pressure just got to be too much.</i>

841
00:54:01,541 --> 00:54:02,958
<i>What pressure do you mean?</i>

842
00:54:06,083 --> 00:54:08,291
<i>The pressure I put on myself.</i>

843
00:54:09,916 --> 00:54:11,708
<i>To be perfect in everything I do.</i>

844
00:54:13,250 --> 00:54:15,625
<i>In the end, I…</i>

845
00:54:17,125 --> 00:54:19,041
I no longer felt anything
when I made music.

846
00:54:22,000 --> 00:54:25,125
I realized I had missed
a lot of what was going on around me.

847
00:54:26,791 --> 00:54:29,625
And that I had been ignoring people.

848
00:54:29,708 --> 00:54:33,666
Sounds like you thought that you had
to sacrifice everything to make music.

849
00:54:36,083 --> 00:54:36,916
Basically.

850
00:54:37,583 --> 00:54:38,708
But maybe…

851
00:54:39,500 --> 00:54:41,208
it's the other way around.

852
00:54:41,750 --> 00:54:47,666
What you've missed out on in life is
what you need to be able to make music.

853
00:54:47,750 --> 00:54:49,041
Do you know what I mean?

854
00:54:53,000 --> 00:54:55,125
Just try not wanting anything for a while.

855
00:55:04,875 --> 00:55:05,916
Hi.

856
00:55:16,083 --> 00:55:19,625
Hey, Oli! What about your student ID?

857
00:55:20,916 --> 00:55:21,958
Stop!

858
00:55:23,708 --> 00:55:24,541
Out of my way.

859
00:55:28,250 --> 00:55:30,291
-Stop!
-Come get me!

860
00:55:31,083 --> 00:55:32,125
Stop!

861
00:55:32,791 --> 00:55:33,750
I told you to…

862
00:55:40,125 --> 00:55:40,958
All right…

863
00:55:43,125 --> 00:55:43,958
Okay.

864
00:56:16,416 --> 00:56:17,541
Get out.

865
00:56:18,208 --> 00:56:20,708
-No, piss off. Please leave.
-Just a moment.

866
00:56:20,791 --> 00:56:23,208
-Why are you here?
-Please!

867
00:56:23,291 --> 00:56:25,000
-Open the door.
-And why?

868
00:56:25,083 --> 00:56:26,041
Are you alone?

869
00:56:26,125 --> 00:56:30,958
Just forget everything I said.
I just wanted to provoke you. I swear.

870
00:56:31,041 --> 00:56:34,250
You can't open the door.
He'll kill me. Please.

871
00:56:34,333 --> 00:56:35,333
Is someone in there?

872
00:56:35,875 --> 00:56:38,041
It's okay. I'm on my own.

873
00:56:38,125 --> 00:56:40,625
-Okay, buddy. I'll find you.
-Okay.

874
00:56:41,791 --> 00:56:42,625
Thanks.

875
00:56:43,125 --> 00:56:44,250
It's okay.

876
00:56:50,458 --> 00:56:52,916
Okay. Are these all yours?

877
00:56:53,541 --> 00:56:54,375
Yes.

878
00:56:55,333 --> 00:56:56,166
Cool.

879
00:56:57,166 --> 00:56:58,458
They're beautiful.

880
00:57:03,916 --> 00:57:05,750
But that's plagiarism.

881
00:57:06,791 --> 00:57:09,958
That's your photo, asshole.
I'm not passing it off as mine.

882
00:57:12,125 --> 00:57:13,333
I want it back.

883
00:57:16,333 --> 00:57:18,125
It's part of my project now.

884
00:57:18,708 --> 00:57:22,833
I think you probably just want…
to have a little memento of me.

885
00:57:24,166 --> 00:57:26,333
You're so conceited.

886
00:57:48,958 --> 00:57:51,250
This doesn't mean that I'm into you.

887
00:57:52,500 --> 00:57:55,291
-You're not my type anyway.
-Oh, really?

888
00:57:55,375 --> 00:57:56,583
You've got a type?

889
00:58:10,125 --> 00:58:11,750
Like anyone with a pulse?

890
00:58:24,125 --> 00:58:25,541
Jamie? You're up.

891
00:58:26,375 --> 00:58:27,208
<i>Jamie.</i>

892
00:58:27,916 --> 00:58:30,208
-We really need to start.
-Yes.

893
00:58:31,041 --> 00:58:32,000
Here you are.

894
00:58:32,916 --> 00:58:34,708
<i>You want to become a doctor?</i>

895
00:58:34,791 --> 00:58:36,958
<i>Well, I wouldn't say "want to."</i>

896
00:58:37,041 --> 00:58:38,583
<i>My dad is also a doctor.</i>

897
00:58:39,125 --> 00:58:40,416
-Really?
-Yes.

898
00:58:40,500 --> 00:58:44,416
I have no idea how to tell him
that I'd prefer to make music.

899
00:58:44,500 --> 00:58:47,166
Your dad decided to be a parent, right?

900
00:58:47,250 --> 00:58:48,958
-Yes.
-Then it's his job to listen.

901
00:58:49,041 --> 00:58:51,333
-What's your name again?
-Chris.

902
00:58:52,458 --> 00:58:54,375
First you play D major.

903
00:58:55,250 --> 00:58:56,250
D major.

904
00:58:56,333 --> 00:58:57,375
Then G.

905
00:58:59,125 --> 00:59:02,916
-Just a bit longer.
-I need to finish the photos. Bye.

906
00:59:03,000 --> 00:59:03,833
Ciao.

907
00:59:21,833 --> 00:59:24,875
And this is our auditorium.

908
00:59:24,958 --> 00:59:28,083
They want to completely modernize it.
Cool, right?

909
00:59:35,666 --> 00:59:36,500
Here.

910
00:59:38,125 --> 00:59:39,750
It's not perfect yet.

911
00:59:40,500 --> 00:59:42,000
It's just a draft.

912
00:59:43,125 --> 00:59:46,583
-The end isn't quite right yet.
-Never mind, just play it.

913
00:59:50,875 --> 00:59:53,000
<i>All of this time</i>
<i>Make it…</i>

914
00:59:55,958 --> 00:59:58,791
<i>All of this time</i>
<i>Make it all make sense</i>

915
00:59:58,875 --> 01:00:03,125
<i>I'm going higher and higher</i>
<i>From Earth into space</i>

916
01:00:03,208 --> 01:00:06,708
<i>It's an existential searching</i>
<i>How it hurts</i>

917
01:00:06,791 --> 01:00:09,833
<i>But you know we gotta make it work</i>

918
01:00:09,916 --> 01:00:12,625
<i>We gotta make it work</i>

919
01:00:14,541 --> 01:00:18,166
-As you can tell, it's not quite…
-It's really good.

920
01:00:18,708 --> 01:00:19,958
Can I try something?

921
01:00:23,750 --> 01:00:24,583
You started by…

922
01:00:26,041 --> 01:00:28,166
-I started here.
-With F. Okay.

923
01:00:44,083 --> 01:00:46,666
-It's really good.
-That was completely different.

924
01:00:48,000 --> 01:00:50,083
Maybe just a little less… intellectual.

925
01:00:51,708 --> 01:00:53,916
-Touché.
-Come on, try it again.

926
01:00:56,875 --> 01:00:59,916
<i>All of this time</i>
<i>Make it all make sense</i>

927
01:01:00,000 --> 01:01:02,041
<i>I'm going round and round</i>

928
01:01:03,416 --> 01:01:05,416
<i>In circles again</i>

929
01:01:05,500 --> 01:01:09,125
<i>It's an existential searching</i>
<i>How it hurts</i>

930
01:01:09,208 --> 01:01:10,833
<i>But you know we gotta make…</i>

931
01:01:10,916 --> 01:01:14,625
You're still trying to do it perfectly.
Now put the notebook away and…

932
01:01:14,708 --> 01:01:16,000
You've got this.

933
01:01:17,083 --> 01:01:19,291
Just see where the music takes you.

934
01:01:20,000 --> 01:01:22,000
-Okay.
-But wait a minute.

935
01:01:25,458 --> 01:01:28,583
-This helps me sometimes.
-What's that supposed to be?

936
01:01:28,666 --> 01:01:30,083
Now, we'll do one…

937
01:01:32,166 --> 01:01:33,166
just…

938
01:01:34,458 --> 01:01:35,750
for us, okay?

939
01:01:40,708 --> 01:01:44,500
First you take my notebook away,
and now you want to record me?

940
01:01:45,208 --> 01:01:46,041
Ready?

941
01:01:48,000 --> 01:01:48,833
Ready.

942
01:01:50,500 --> 01:01:51,500
Okay.

943
01:02:02,375 --> 01:02:05,250
<i>If I could turn back time</i>

944
01:02:05,875 --> 01:02:09,416
<i>And relive these crazy memories</i>

945
01:02:10,291 --> 01:02:13,583
<i>Back to the people that we used to be</i>

946
01:02:15,000 --> 01:02:17,250
<i>Travel there with me</i>

947
01:02:17,916 --> 01:02:20,750
<i>If we could turn back the clock</i>

948
01:02:21,916 --> 01:02:25,416
<i>Someone tell me where we're gonna go</i>

949
01:02:26,000 --> 01:02:28,708
<i>Open the curtain to a world</i>

950
01:02:28,791 --> 01:02:30,958
<i>That we've been dreaming of</i>

951
01:02:32,166 --> 01:02:36,333
<i>That we've been dreaming of</i>

952
01:03:37,666 --> 01:03:39,791
I wish we had more time together.

953
01:03:41,833 --> 01:03:42,833
But we do.

954
01:03:52,625 --> 01:03:53,458
Again?

955
01:03:56,083 --> 01:03:56,916
No!

956
01:03:57,458 --> 01:03:59,166
-Shit!
-Is everything okay?

957
01:03:59,250 --> 01:04:01,791
I forgot about the presentation. Shit.

958
01:04:02,375 --> 01:04:04,208
Jamie is gonna kill me.

959
01:04:05,208 --> 01:04:07,375
I'm sorry, I have to go.

960
01:04:12,583 --> 01:04:14,583
-Good luck.
-Thank you.

961
01:04:35,833 --> 01:04:39,875
<i>If I could take us right back</i>
<i>Back from here to the start</i>

962
01:04:40,666 --> 01:04:42,875
<i>The look you gave me</i>

963
01:04:44,041 --> 01:04:46,458
<i>By the end of the party</i>

964
01:04:49,583 --> 01:04:52,291
The look you gave me
by the end of the party…

965
01:05:06,583 --> 01:05:09,083
-I think I want to stay here.
-What?

966
01:05:09,166 --> 01:05:11,500
-Are you crazy?
-It's so different for me here.

967
01:05:11,583 --> 01:05:14,625
-Yes, it's the '90s.
-No, not because of that.

968
01:05:15,250 --> 01:05:18,916
I just feel free for the first time.
With my music. With Mary Jane.

969
01:05:19,000 --> 01:05:22,166
-Okay, great. He's in love.
-This can't be happening.

970
01:05:22,250 --> 01:05:24,750
Bro, this isn't some kind
of self-discovery trip to Bali.

971
01:05:24,833 --> 01:05:25,875
I know that.

972
01:05:25,958 --> 01:05:28,250
-What happened with Mary Jane?
-Nothing.

973
01:05:28,333 --> 01:05:30,750
I see, you just want to stay
to watch some MTV?

974
01:05:31,333 --> 01:05:32,166
No.

975
01:05:33,416 --> 01:05:36,583
We played some music together
and then we kissed.

976
01:05:36,666 --> 01:05:40,708
You made out with her?
I told you not to make out with anyone.

977
01:05:40,791 --> 01:05:44,166
Ben, really. That's not okay.
So embarrassing.

978
01:05:44,250 --> 01:05:47,750
-Not cool, bro.
-You know what? I'm not even mad.

979
01:05:48,416 --> 01:05:50,666
-I'm just disappointed.
-Come on, guys.

980
01:05:51,541 --> 01:05:52,416
Wait a minute.

981
01:05:53,041 --> 01:05:56,666
There's that basketball player
in the background. Charlie Crawford with…

982
01:05:56,750 --> 01:05:59,000
-That's Mary Jane, right?
-Yes.

983
01:05:59,083 --> 01:06:00,916
Are they a couple? They're fighting.

984
01:06:01,000 --> 01:06:03,125
-100%. I can see that.
-Yes, right?

985
01:06:03,208 --> 01:06:05,708
-They're not a good match.
-Hang on.

986
01:06:05,791 --> 01:06:08,208
Mary Jane wrote that song
about Charlie Crawford.

987
01:06:08,291 --> 01:06:10,541
<i>-What? They were a couple?</i>
<i>-As if.</i>

988
01:06:10,916 --> 01:06:11,916
No way!

989
01:06:12,000 --> 01:06:13,083
Yes, exactly.

990
01:06:13,166 --> 01:06:15,166
<i>Charlie C. cheated on Mary Jane.</i>

991
01:06:16,208 --> 01:06:18,125
They can't control their balls.

992
01:06:19,291 --> 01:06:21,833
<i>And she wrote a song</i>
<i>to process the breakup.</i>

993
01:06:21,916 --> 01:06:24,208
<i>So, the song is about Charlie Crawford.</i>

994
01:06:25,458 --> 01:06:27,958
<i>Then she submitted it to </i>Submission Hour.

995
01:06:28,750 --> 01:06:29,833
<i>And, as we saw,</i>

996
01:06:29,916 --> 01:06:31,125
<i>the song was well received</i>

997
01:06:31,208 --> 01:06:33,125
<i>and that was somehow too much for her,</i>

998
01:06:33,208 --> 01:06:34,833
<i>so she stole it back.</i>

999
01:06:34,916 --> 01:06:37,500
It was stolen. Breakup song
They must've regretted it.

1000
01:06:38,250 --> 01:06:40,958
<i>Mary Jane must have the song on tape.</i>

1001
01:06:44,916 --> 01:06:47,916
-Could be.
-Yes, dude. Totally.

1002
01:06:51,208 --> 01:06:54,875
Dude, that's the tape. Why didn't you say?
"Charlie C." We've got it.

1003
01:06:55,500 --> 01:06:57,041
-Let's go home.
-Yes.

1004
01:07:00,083 --> 01:07:01,416
-Yes!
-Yes, cool.

1005
01:07:01,500 --> 01:07:04,000
-We're going to make it.
-How cool is that?

1006
01:07:04,083 --> 01:07:06,208
-Perfect, let's…
-Wait.

1007
01:07:06,625 --> 01:07:08,458
-What is that?
-That's not the song.

1008
01:07:10,583 --> 01:07:14,125
-Ben, why is your voice on the tape?
-This can't be happening.

1009
01:07:14,208 --> 01:07:16,916
<i>Dreaming of</i>

1010
01:07:19,750 --> 01:07:23,958
Did you really destroy
our only chance to get back home?

1011
01:07:24,541 --> 01:07:28,041
-I didn't do it on purpose.
-You just said you wanted to stay.

1012
01:07:28,125 --> 01:07:31,750
-Was this your plan?
-Did you think we'd be okay staying here?

1013
01:07:31,833 --> 01:07:34,208
How was I supposed to know
the song would be taped over?

1014
01:07:34,291 --> 01:07:37,458
-Bullshit!
-Exactly the song that we need, huh?

1015
01:07:37,541 --> 01:07:41,708
Or you wanted to keep us here. To have
a happy ending with your retro love.

1016
01:07:41,791 --> 01:07:42,625
Thanks.

1017
01:07:42,708 --> 01:07:44,875
We're all screwed because of you.

1018
01:07:45,500 --> 01:07:46,708
Thanks for nothing.

1019
01:07:47,458 --> 01:07:49,666
Guys, please. Just wait.

1020
01:08:07,166 --> 01:08:08,833
We'll never get back home now.

1021
01:08:14,916 --> 01:08:15,750
Wait a minute.

1022
01:08:18,208 --> 01:08:20,208
What was it that you said?

1023
01:08:24,291 --> 01:08:25,750
"Nothing you hear

1024
01:08:27,000 --> 01:08:28,333
will show you the way."

1025
01:08:29,125 --> 01:08:30,333
"Only what remains will."

1026
01:08:31,083 --> 01:08:32,583
"Silent and abundant."

1027
01:08:34,333 --> 01:08:36,708
"What sounds devoid of truth

1028
01:08:37,333 --> 01:08:38,500
moves nothing forward."

1029
01:08:38,583 --> 01:08:40,250
"And nothing will succeed."

1030
01:08:42,750 --> 01:08:45,166
"Only what you truly feel and show

1031
01:08:45,708 --> 01:08:48,041
opens doors that others don't know."

1032
01:08:54,208 --> 01:08:55,333
So, you mean,

1033
01:08:55,958 --> 01:08:57,583
we just need a song that's…

1034
01:08:58,125 --> 01:08:58,958
That's real?

1035
01:08:59,541 --> 01:09:02,208
No. You need a pan flute solo.

1036
01:09:03,666 --> 01:09:05,250
Of course, that's what I mean.

1037
01:09:11,000 --> 01:09:11,833
I've got it.

1038
01:09:13,666 --> 01:09:14,625
Yes, I've got it.

1039
01:09:20,083 --> 01:09:22,791
Mary Jane wrote the song
about Charlie Crawford.

1040
01:09:34,458 --> 01:09:36,875
Mary Jane wrote the song.

1041
01:09:41,500 --> 01:09:42,875
Oh, my…

1042
01:09:50,041 --> 01:09:53,166
Jamie! How did it go?
Did you get the funding?

1043
01:09:53,916 --> 01:09:55,708
-Yes.
-Cool!

1044
01:09:59,000 --> 01:10:00,583
Have you heard the latest?

1045
01:10:00,666 --> 01:10:02,583
I shouldn't have let you down.

1046
01:10:03,833 --> 01:10:08,791
I know you have big plans for us,
but my heart just isn't in it right now.

1047
01:10:10,208 --> 01:10:13,125
The song by Mary Jane was about Charlie C.

1048
01:10:14,000 --> 01:10:15,750
Mary Jane wrote it.

1049
01:10:18,750 --> 01:10:22,000
Mary Jane's song was about Charlie C.
They were dating.

1050
01:10:26,291 --> 01:10:27,833
I want to study music.

1051
01:10:28,375 --> 01:10:29,458
For real.

1052
01:10:32,000 --> 01:10:34,208
It's taken you long enough!

1053
01:10:34,291 --> 01:10:38,333
I've been waiting forever for you
to tell me what you really want.

1054
01:10:42,708 --> 01:10:45,333
Do you remember our favorite song?

1055
01:10:45,416 --> 01:10:48,625
I know who wrote it. More importantly,
who the song is about.

1056
01:10:51,166 --> 01:10:52,583
I like your song.

1057
01:10:53,125 --> 01:10:55,125
<i>I still hear you</i>

1058
01:10:55,208 --> 01:10:59,166
<i>In the midnight air</i>

1059
01:10:59,666 --> 01:11:02,250
<i>Your voice is the wind</i>
<i>It's the sound…</i>

1060
01:11:02,333 --> 01:11:04,166
Oh, shit, that's my song.

1061
01:11:04,250 --> 01:11:06,875
Nobody was meant to know that I wrote it.

1062
01:11:06,958 --> 01:11:09,125
It doesn't matter. You're so talented.

1063
01:11:09,625 --> 01:11:10,666
Oh, my God.

1064
01:11:12,083 --> 01:11:15,833
Hey. A computer science student
with a voice like that? Everyone loves it.

1065
01:11:17,250 --> 01:11:19,875
Let me tell you.
We need to do a whole show.

1066
01:11:19,958 --> 01:11:22,250
With only songs by Mary Jane.

1067
01:11:22,333 --> 01:11:24,750
<i>Drift through the night, through the dark</i>

1068
01:11:24,833 --> 01:11:26,708
<i>Where we used to be</i>

1069
01:11:34,750 --> 01:11:36,041
Guys!

1070
01:11:36,666 --> 01:11:38,041
What does he want now?

1071
01:11:38,125 --> 01:11:42,000
There you are. I know how we can
get back home. Come with me.

1072
01:11:42,625 --> 01:11:46,833
-None of us want to go with you.
-Just trust me. Just this once.

1073
01:11:46,916 --> 01:11:48,375
I'll explain everything.

1074
01:11:48,458 --> 01:11:51,083
-And if he's right?
-Okay, come on. Let's go!

1075
01:11:52,708 --> 01:11:55,291
-Oli? Who are they?
-We have to go.

1076
01:11:55,375 --> 01:11:57,708
-Johnny, get them!
-Run!

1077
01:12:43,125 --> 01:12:45,541
No, Daniel! Don't get in!

1078
01:12:57,625 --> 01:12:59,750
Mary Jane.

1079
01:13:18,791 --> 01:13:22,333
-Guys, get in.
-We have to go.

1080
01:13:22,416 --> 01:13:24,750
-Come!
-Ben, that's enough. Get in.

1081
01:13:27,750 --> 01:13:28,875
Come on now!

1082
01:13:32,750 --> 01:13:34,625
See you later, okay?

1083
01:13:38,041 --> 01:13:38,875
You're right.

1084
01:13:44,333 --> 01:13:45,916
See you later.

1085
01:14:04,250 --> 01:14:08,333
No matter what happens now,
you should know that I'm really sorry.

1086
01:14:08,416 --> 01:14:09,750
I behaved like an idiot.

1087
01:14:09,833 --> 01:14:13,166
My obsession for everything to be perfect
made me forget what matters.

1088
01:14:14,083 --> 01:14:15,250
-I'm sorry.
-It's okay, bro.

1089
01:14:15,333 --> 01:14:17,083
-We're all idiots sometimes.
-Not everyone.

1090
01:14:17,166 --> 01:14:19,500
-Thanks, Ben.
-Come here.

1091
01:14:20,791 --> 01:14:21,625
Oh, guys…

1092
01:14:22,583 --> 01:14:23,625
I love you guys.

1093
01:14:26,416 --> 01:14:27,583
So, what's the plan?

1094
01:14:28,375 --> 01:14:29,958
This should work.

1095
01:14:30,041 --> 01:14:32,041
-The janitor said…
-Wait, what janitor?

1096
01:14:32,125 --> 01:14:35,041
Never mind. We don't need
that one special song.

1097
01:14:35,791 --> 01:14:38,125
Just a song that was created
with true feeling.

1098
01:14:38,208 --> 01:14:40,958
Ben, we only have seconds,
but take your time.

1099
01:14:41,041 --> 01:14:43,791
We just need a song that's real.

1100
01:14:43,875 --> 01:14:47,666
The song I recorded
with Mary Jane is more than real.

1101
01:14:48,250 --> 01:14:49,541
-It should work.
-Should?

1102
01:14:49,625 --> 01:14:51,208
Or we'll crash with the elevator.

1103
01:14:51,291 --> 01:14:53,375
Great. I'd rather stay here than die.

1104
01:14:53,458 --> 01:14:56,250
-You want to live without your phone?
-Damn. Good point.

1105
01:15:00,958 --> 01:15:02,791
-Okay, let's go.
-Let's try.

1106
01:15:04,083 --> 01:15:05,291
Okay, play.

1107
01:15:06,583 --> 01:15:07,416
What?

1108
01:15:11,333 --> 01:15:12,166
Shit!

1109
01:15:15,041 --> 01:15:16,666
Oh, my God!

1110
01:15:37,250 --> 01:15:39,791
<i>Travel there with me</i>

1111
01:15:40,708 --> 01:15:43,333
<i>If we could turn back the clock</i>

1112
01:15:44,166 --> 01:15:47,541
<i>Someone tell me where we're gonna go</i>

1113
01:15:48,250 --> 01:15:51,208
<i>Open a curtain to a world</i>

1114
01:15:51,291 --> 01:15:53,500
<i>That we've been dreaming of</i>

1115
01:15:54,625 --> 01:15:57,875
<i>That we've been dreaming of</i>

1116
01:16:28,333 --> 01:16:31,000
Oh, my God. My phone's working again.

1117
01:16:32,208 --> 01:16:34,333
-Yes!
-We're back in the present.

1118
01:16:35,000 --> 01:16:36,750
-Oh, my God.
-Thank God.

1119
01:16:36,833 --> 01:16:39,916
And… And we're 5G again.

1120
01:16:40,000 --> 01:16:41,666
It's kind of empty here, isn't it?

1121
01:16:42,250 --> 01:16:44,458
That's right,
the showcase is on right now.

1122
01:16:45,416 --> 01:16:46,625
Then let's perform.

1123
01:16:48,000 --> 01:16:49,083
What do you mean?

1124
01:16:49,166 --> 01:16:51,750
-We don't even have a song.
-You said the song is shit.

1125
01:16:52,500 --> 01:16:53,916
We've got a new song.

1126
01:16:54,458 --> 01:16:55,458
Come with me.

1127
01:16:56,166 --> 01:16:57,500
The back entrance!

1128
01:16:57,583 --> 01:16:58,666
Quick, run!

1129
01:17:05,541 --> 01:17:07,750
Shit! The showcase is over.

1130
01:17:08,291 --> 01:17:09,291
Never mind, go in.

1131
01:17:10,250 --> 01:17:15,041
<i>…and someone might be</i>
<i>cheering much louder than everyone else.</i>

1132
01:17:15,750 --> 01:17:20,166
<i>Before we find out who won, we need</i>
<i>the woman who'll make the decision.</i>

1133
01:17:21,041 --> 01:17:24,291
<i>The woman with the baton…</i>

1134
01:17:24,375 --> 01:17:27,291
Okay, Ms. Robinson can come on stage now.

1135
01:17:27,375 --> 01:17:30,833
-It's our turn to perform now.
-No, the showcase is over.

1136
01:17:31,500 --> 01:17:33,208
-No, but…
<i>-Ms. Robinson!</i>

1137
01:17:33,291 --> 01:17:36,041
-Shall we?
-Sure. Let's go.

1138
01:17:37,916 --> 01:17:41,250
-There's another act?
-There shouldn't be.

1139
01:17:57,000 --> 01:17:58,500
We're 5G.

1140
01:17:58,583 --> 01:18:01,125
And this is our song, "1995."

1141
01:18:15,166 --> 01:18:18,208
<i>If we could turn back time</i>

1142
01:18:18,875 --> 01:18:22,458
<i>And relive these crazy memories</i>

1143
01:18:23,208 --> 01:18:26,625
<i>Back to the people that we used to be</i>

1144
01:18:27,500 --> 01:18:29,916
<i>Travel there with me</i>

1145
01:18:30,000 --> 01:18:32,541
<i>If we could turn back the clock</i>

1146
01:18:34,125 --> 01:18:37,541
<i>Someone tell me where the years have gone</i>

1147
01:18:38,041 --> 01:18:40,625
<i>Open the curtain to a world</i>

1148
01:18:40,708 --> 01:18:43,208
<i>That we've been dreaming of</i>

1149
01:18:45,041 --> 01:18:47,958
<i>That we've been dreaming of</i>

1150
01:18:59,500 --> 01:19:03,083
<i>If we could turn back time</i>

1151
01:19:03,500 --> 01:19:07,458
<i>Back when we were all together</i>
<i>Day and night</i>

1152
01:19:08,000 --> 01:19:11,625
<i>Talking and laughing</i>
<i>Dreaming how it would be</i>

1153
01:19:11,708 --> 01:19:14,000
<i>If we ever made it</i>

1154
01:19:14,083 --> 01:19:17,875
<i>If we could turn back time</i>

1155
01:19:17,958 --> 01:19:22,333
<i>All those years</i>
<i>In just a blink of our eyes</i>

1156
01:19:23,125 --> 01:19:25,041
<i>Embracing friends for life</i>

1157
01:19:25,125 --> 01:19:28,291
<i>And making up for the time we wasted</i>

1158
01:19:29,416 --> 01:19:32,541
<i>Was it so far back?</i>

1159
01:19:32,625 --> 01:19:35,541
<i>Have we been gone too long?</i>

1160
01:19:36,833 --> 01:19:39,125
<i>If I could take you right back</i>

1161
01:19:39,208 --> 01:19:40,875
<i>Back from here to the start</i>

1162
01:19:40,958 --> 01:19:44,375
<i>We'd party all night</i>
<i>In the back of the car</i>

1163
01:19:44,458 --> 01:19:46,833
<i>Life was totally instinctual</i>

1164
01:19:46,916 --> 01:19:48,750
<i>Fear was invisible</i>

1165
01:19:48,833 --> 01:19:50,875
<i>Didn't we have it all?</i>

1166
01:19:50,958 --> 01:19:54,500
<i>In 1995</i>

1167
01:19:55,000 --> 01:19:58,333
<i>In 1995</i>

1168
01:19:58,416 --> 01:20:02,333
<i>1995</i>

1169
01:20:02,416 --> 01:20:06,500
<i>1995</i>

1170
01:20:06,583 --> 01:20:08,541
<i>If I could take you right back</i>

1171
01:20:08,625 --> 01:20:10,625
<i>Back from here to the start</i>

1172
01:20:10,708 --> 01:20:13,833
<i>We'd party all night</i>
<i>In the back of the car</i>

1173
01:20:13,916 --> 01:20:16,375
<i>Life was totally instinctual</i>

1174
01:20:16,458 --> 01:20:18,500
<i>Fear was invisible</i>

1175
01:20:18,583 --> 01:20:20,458
<i>Didn't we have it all?</i>

1176
01:20:20,875 --> 01:20:24,541
<i>In 1995</i>

1177
01:21:09,625 --> 01:21:11,083
That was really cool.

1178
01:21:34,500 --> 01:21:35,708
Ms. Robinson.

1179
01:21:36,291 --> 01:21:37,291
Excuse me.

1180
01:21:39,625 --> 01:21:41,708
We shouldn't have performed.

1181
01:21:43,875 --> 01:21:45,583
But we gave it our all.

1182
01:21:48,375 --> 01:21:49,375
The song…

1183
01:21:51,333 --> 01:21:52,875
is really close to my heart.

1184
01:21:54,791 --> 01:21:56,125
I know that, Ben.

1185
01:22:08,333 --> 01:22:10,083
I wrote that song with you.

1186
01:22:36,833 --> 01:22:38,375
I just had to come back.

1187
01:22:43,291 --> 01:22:45,750
Maybe we were meant to meet each other.

1188
01:22:49,291 --> 01:22:51,041
Maybe we needed each other.

1189
01:22:58,041 --> 01:23:00,208
Maybe this is a second chance.

1190
01:23:08,291 --> 01:23:10,875
Some things just belong
in the moment they happened.

1191
01:23:13,041 --> 01:23:15,208
But you showed me that love exists.

1192
01:23:16,625 --> 01:23:17,666
<i>That remains.</i>

1193
01:23:19,875 --> 01:23:21,583
<i>Even if you have to let it go.</i>

1194
01:23:24,833 --> 01:23:28,000
I just wish we could have spent
more time together.

1195
01:23:30,416 --> 01:23:31,708
A heart heals, Ben.

1196
01:23:33,583 --> 01:23:35,166
Not right away…

1197
01:23:37,000 --> 01:23:38,958
but it puts itself back together again.

1198
01:23:41,083 --> 01:23:42,458
And keeps beating…

1199
01:23:44,833 --> 01:23:46,458
Stronger than before.

1200
01:24:09,583 --> 01:24:12,625
I've seen many fantastic,
talented artists today.

1201
01:24:14,625 --> 01:24:16,541
But talent alone is not enough.

1202
01:24:18,750 --> 01:24:22,208
It takes courage to go your own way.

1203
01:24:23,083 --> 01:24:24,000
Dad.

1204
01:24:26,000 --> 01:24:28,125
Come here. I'm so proud of you.

1205
01:24:31,208 --> 01:24:33,875
Sometimes, we're so focused on our goal

1206
01:24:35,208 --> 01:24:38,250
that we forget what truly drives us.

1207
01:24:40,916 --> 01:24:42,583
You're not mad at me?

1208
01:24:43,708 --> 01:24:44,708
Why?

1209
01:24:44,791 --> 01:24:47,166
About the interview with Mehackerberg?

1210
01:24:47,875 --> 01:24:48,708
The letter?

1211
01:24:48,791 --> 01:24:52,083
I think you need to calm down first.
You're a little flustered.

1212
01:24:59,875 --> 01:25:01,875
Life should inspire art.

1213
01:25:04,416 --> 01:25:05,708
Not the other way around.

1214
01:25:08,375 --> 01:25:10,833
Those who don't experience anything
have nothing to share.

1215
01:25:12,625 --> 01:25:14,250
And I saw that courage

1216
01:25:15,333 --> 01:25:17,333
in someone today.

1217
01:25:18,833 --> 01:25:21,833
Someone who was willing to open up
and show themselves.

1218
01:25:22,583 --> 01:25:23,750
Without fear.

1219
01:25:24,791 --> 01:25:26,833
But with real passion.

1220
01:25:29,250 --> 01:25:31,083
That's why I'd like

1221
01:25:31,666 --> 01:25:34,875
to give Skyler the chance she deserves.

1222
01:26:37,666 --> 01:26:39,458
You know what's great about this morning?

1223
01:26:39,541 --> 01:26:41,333
It's not that the birds are singing.

1224
01:26:41,416 --> 01:26:44,458
It's the fact that we didn't have
to bring any pink sprinkles.

1225
01:26:45,083 --> 01:26:46,208
I knew you'd say that.

1226
01:26:46,291 --> 01:26:50,291
You know what else is great? For once
being well-rested for a rehearsal.

1227
01:26:50,375 --> 01:26:54,666
Yes. Do you know why?
Because we finally got enough sleep.

1228
01:26:54,750 --> 01:26:58,916
I'm happy that Ben doesn't kick in
the door like a SWAT team anymore.

1229
01:26:59,000 --> 01:27:02,125
You used to really freak out
when we weren't here by 6:00 a.m.

1230
01:27:02,208 --> 01:27:04,166
-Don't exaggerate.
-Exaggerate?

1231
01:27:04,250 --> 01:27:07,333
You once texted me at 3:00 a.m.
reminding me to learn my lyrics.

1232
01:27:07,416 --> 01:27:08,833
With a skull emoji.

1233
01:27:08,916 --> 01:27:11,666
-I just wanted to motivate you.
-More like "manipulate."

1234
01:27:14,625 --> 01:27:15,791
But honestly, Ben,

1235
01:27:16,333 --> 01:27:19,916
you're so relaxed now,
it's almost pleasant.

1236
01:27:20,000 --> 01:27:23,333
I don't know what you mean.
I have always been very relaxed.

1237
01:27:23,416 --> 01:27:26,750
-Yes! 100%.
-Look me in the eyes and say that again.

1238
01:27:26,833 --> 01:27:28,833
I've just always been very committed.

1239
01:27:29,666 --> 01:27:33,791
A follower just sent me a link to a TikTok
account full of cool vintage B-sides.

1240
01:27:33,875 --> 01:27:35,333
-Don't!
-Oli, stop!

1241
01:27:40,875 --> 01:27:42,041
Slate 2, take 2.

1242
01:27:44,833 --> 01:27:48,791
We don't get involved with anyone,
we don't "pefriend" anyone.

1243
01:27:48,875 --> 01:27:50,041
Sorry.

1244
01:27:50,125 --> 01:27:51,458
The rules are as follows.

1245
01:27:53,083 --> 01:27:54,208
I'm sorry, I can't…

1246
01:27:54,291 --> 01:27:55,333
Action.

1247
01:27:58,375 --> 01:28:00,125
I was startled there.

1248
01:28:00,208 --> 01:28:01,958
Jonas, open the door.

1249
01:28:02,041 --> 01:28:03,666
It's Ben. Go again.

1250
01:28:03,750 --> 01:28:04,791
It's always the same.

1251
01:28:05,958 --> 01:28:08,375
{\an8}-On the mark.
-I'm not on the mark?

1252
01:28:08,458 --> 01:28:10,041
{\an8}I'll start over…

1253
01:28:10,833 --> 01:28:13,250
Well, when I heard… Shit!

1254
01:28:13,333 --> 01:28:15,250
-Forgot the line.
-I returned it though.

1255
01:28:15,333 --> 01:28:16,166
Sorry.

1256
01:28:16,875 --> 01:28:19,750
What did you say? "She should piss off?
Kiss my ass?"

1257
01:28:19,833 --> 01:28:21,708
-She should piss off.
-Kids.

1258
01:28:39,666 --> 01:28:40,791
<i>Hello, you.</i>

1259
01:28:46,333 --> 01:28:48,875
You just hit me in the face so hard.

1260
01:28:48,958 --> 01:28:50,750
-How's it going?
-It's not a talk show.

1261
01:28:50,833 --> 01:28:52,166
-Makeup?
-Where have you been?

1262
01:28:52,250 --> 01:28:54,583
-In the bathroom.
-How was it?

1263
01:28:54,666 --> 01:28:57,375
-Smooth.
-Great. And we're smoothly moving on.

1264
01:28:57,458 --> 01:28:58,416
The nudist beach?

1265
01:29:02,583 --> 01:29:03,458
Yes, but…

1266
01:29:05,208 --> 01:29:06,416
Have I forgotten anything?

1267
01:29:08,083 --> 01:29:09,625
Okay, Charlie is tired.

1268
01:29:11,083 --> 01:29:13,916
-What's that?
-It's my Tamagotchi.

1269
01:29:14,458 --> 01:29:15,291
And cut.